{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play","id":356914,"title":"Freehold of the Palisades Setting / Actual Play","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-01T14:29:07-08:00","id":356914,"title":"Freehold of the Palisades Setting / Actual Play","content":"Inspired by Baron Samedi's excellent Malibu Dream House actual play thread, I've decided that I want to try my hand at Storytelling a Changeling game, starting from the ground up. At the moment I am looking for players in my local game store, and in the meantime I am developing the setting. I welcome all comments on the game, especially as a new Storyteller to the setting. My one small caveat is that I have more or less no problem with &quot;out of time&quot; characters, either as NPCs or as PCs, so long as they don't become overwhelming. It is going to be a settled part of the game that these sorts of Changelings, though rare, do exist. I only bring this up because, from my on-line research, it seems to be a contentious issue!<br /> <br /> If you like anything from the setting or game, please feel free to steal it.<br /> <br /> The game will take place in the Freehold of the Palisades, which covers northern and central New Jersey following this map.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=77\" data-tempid=\"temp_77_1420149659371_589\" /><br /> <br /> I've written up a history of the Freehold as a starting point both for myself and my players. Here it is for your perusal:<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Freehold of the Palisades History</span></b><br /> <br /> North/Central New Jersey<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>Before 1776</i></span><br /> <br /> Almost nothing is known of how Changelings in northern and central New Jersey organized themselves before December 26, 1776. On the day after Christmas, George Washington crossed the Delaware river and attacked the Crown's Hessian mercenaries in what would become known as the Battle of Trenton.<br /> <br /> Whatever structure pre-revolutionary Changelings had fell on that day, leaving nothing but a about half a dozen written records, mostly in the form of journal scraps. These journals, mostly containing personal information of little use today, indicate that something like the standard Western European seasonal model prevailed in the area.<br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"font-size:12px\">1776-1812</span></i><br /> <br /> Following the Battle of Trenton, a Changeling Hessian mercenary known today only as Obrist &quot;Colonel&quot; Wei\u00dfekopf survived by fleeing into the wilderness. The Continental army never found him, and Wei\u00dfekop took it upon himself to reconstitute a Freehold for Changelings in the area. Wei\u00dfekop had served as mercenary previously for some Polish szlachta, and his time spent in Poland saw him abandon the Summer Court of his earlier years for the Sun Court that was especially prevalent in the eastern reaches of the Polish Commonwealth.<br /> <br /> Wei\u00dfekop sensed an opportunity to spread the diurnal Court system to an area dominated by the seasonal Courts. Changeling historical scholars surmise that Wei\u00dfekop gathered the remnants of local Summer and Winter courtiers to form his new Sun Court. The founding document of the court, named the Noontime Declaration, was signed and sealed as a pledge between the founders of the new Freehold of the Palisades on Kupala Day (according to Polish custom June 23-24, the pact was signed at noon of the 23rd) 1777, near what would become Fort Lee, New Jersey.Colonel Wei\u00dfekopf was named King of the Sun and General of the Noontime Pact. Neither the title of General nor that of King stuck to Wei\u00dfekop, and historical records continue to refer to him as a Colonel. Six other names appeared on the Noontime Declaration; nothing is known about 5 of these Changelings other than their names: Probus, William Gafferty, Smiling Henry, Frederick Carthan, and Paul Plooster. The sixth name (and only woman), Wendy Bath, is mentioned in one of the pre-1776 journal fragments as being the Winter Queen of &quot;East New Jersey.&quot; The same journal names her a &quot;damnable Witchtooth,&quot; indicating her Kith and likely Seeming.<br /> <br /> The Noontime Declaration makes no provision for a Moon Court to compliment the Sun Court. While this gave the Freehold of the Palisades unprecedented political unity, the mystical symbolism of the day turning into night and vice versa was missing. This symbolism is, amongst other things, used to confuse the Kindly Ones and prevent them from noticing Changelings pledged to a diurnal-Court ruled Freehold. Consequently, tales of loyalists, privateers, and even True Fae troubling the Freehold from this period are significantly higher than in other Freeholds of the same time period. The Palisades would have to wait until the War of 1812 before a proper Moon Court would be established.<br /> <br /> <i>Known Seemings/Kiths of Historical Figures in this Era:</i><br /> <br /> Colonel Wei\u00dfekopf- Elemental Airtouched<br /> <br /> Wendy Bath- Ogre Witchtooth<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>1812 - 1877</i></span><br /> <br /> Shortly before the beginning of the War of 1812, in 1810, Colonel Wei\u00dfekopf abdicated as King of the Sun. Officialy, the retirement was due to age. The Colonel's inability to control True Fae incursions into the Freehold, coupled with his refusal to allow a Moon Court to &quot;fill out&quot; the magical protections of the diurnal Court system, likely contributed to his ouster. He passed on the title King of the Sun to his successor, who promptly decalred that the title General of the Noontime Pact was unique to Wei\u00dfekopf and no Changeling in the Freehold could even claim it again. Colonel Wei\u00dfekopf faded then from the records, mostly, until 1817, when his funeral was recorded as being well-attended.<br /> <br /> Wei\u00dfekopf's successor was a Changeling known as Gerald Baines, who served in the human world as an Episcopalian vicar in Philipsburg Township (later Philipsburg) in Warren Counry. Baines assumed the title King of the Sun, and already had the title Fae Bishop of Belvidere from Wei\u00dfekopf, which he kept as his secondary title. During his rule he was mostly known as Bishop Baines or Bishop Gerald.<br /> <br /> Baines was a compromise candidated between the Freehold members that wanted Wei\u00dfekopf out and those who supported him, as Baines was well regarded as being a paragon of Sun virtues, but relatively uninterested in Freehold politics. As it turns out, while Baines was personally loyal to Wei\u00dfekopf and his memory, he understood the need to have a Moon Court established in the Freehold. His goal was to do this by finding a politically weak person that could become King of the Moon without much actual power. This would, he hoped, complete the diurnal symbolism for the Wyrd's sake, but without causing all of the political and cultural friction that occured in most diurnal Freeholds.<br /> <br /> Though there were a good number of courtless in the Freehold who had the proper attitude and interestes to be come the Moon monarch, Baines preferred an outsider or newcomer who would be politically weak. The opportunity for this came during the War of 1812, when the British captured and sunk a number of a ships along the New Jersey coast between 1813 and 1814. In addition to the mundane sailors that washed up on the shore, this period saw a large number of new Changeling arrivals from Hedge. The records indicate that one of these refugees, named Zebulon Weeks, declared himself King of the Moon and Envoy of Scorpio in 1817, attracting his first courtiers from many of the courtless involved in criminal activities or otherwise not welcome in the Sun Court.<br /> <br /> There is no record of King Zebulon ever meeting with Bishop Baines, but unlike Wei\u00dfekopf who drove off or killed anyone attempting to claim royal Lunar authority, no response came from Baines. The Moon Court was finally established. Within 7 years, the amount of troubles coming from the Hedge and Arcadia fell off significantly. Whether through coincidence or secret compact, King Zebulon troubled the Sun Court and its subjects only just enough to keep up appearances, and tried to make his courtiers do the same. This created a lot of grumbling in the Moon Court, but Weeks managed to keep it under control until 1877. In that year, a bloody coup saw King Zebulon overthrown and replaced by Bogumil Sobczak in an event known as the Setting of the Scorpion. Many of Zebulon's supporters were killed and the King himself was forced to suddenly flee into the west (never to be seen again).<br /> <br /> Sobczak was an immigrant from eastern Poland, a pagan, and use to a much more aggressive stance from the Moon Court toward their sunny brethren. Much to Sobczak's delight, Bishop Baines and the Sun Court were shocked and disgusted by the coup.<br /> <br /> In the latter part of this period, the American Civil War occured. While the war certainly affected the personal lives of many Changelings, it did not have a large political impact on the Freehold because all of the war was fought in the South. In fact, the period right after the Civil War was a bit of a windfall of Glamour for the Sun Court, since many, many soldiers returned, their souls burdened with shame and guilt over things they had done during the fighting.<br /> <br /> <i>Known Seemings/Kiths of Historical Figures in this Era:</i><br /> <br /> Bishop Gerald Baines- Beast Truefriend<br /> <br /> King Zebulon Weeks- Dual Kith Beast Venombite/Truefriend.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>1877 - the Great War</i></span><br /> <br /> Sobczak assumed the title of King of the Moon and created for himself the secondary title of Supreme Volhv of Chernobog. He was mostly known as King Bogumil. King Bogumil greatly escalated the cold war between Sun and Moon to mirror what he was accustomed to in Poland. This put Baines and the Sun Court on their collective back foot, as they were used to the relatively quiescent Moon Court under Weeks.<br /> <br /> The sudden degradation in relations between the two courts upset the humors of the already elderly Bishop Baines, and he took ill 7 years after Sobczak's rise to power. Unable to get out of bed most days, a regent was appointed to govern in his stead. This regent took the form of Reeve Vauxhall, Baines's secretary and a Magistrate of the Wax Mask. Regent Vauxhall was unpopular with the Sun courtiers, first of all because his Manikin features combined with the melting wax face granted by his Entitlement created a highly disturbing visage, and secondarily because of his brusque and unfriendly manner. He was tolerated, however, because he enjoyed the direct confidence of Bishop Baines (who was well loved) and his refusal to take any gruff from King Bogumil and his court of grotesque horrors.<br /> <br /> The conflicts between the Sun and Moon courts during this period are too numerous to list. Bishop Baines finally expired the day before Kupala Day (June 22nd) in 1900. Regent Vauxhall's long period of rule had left the court with no clear successor, so Vauxhall continued as regent without claiming royal perogatives. This proved to be mystically disatrous, because without a royal figure, the Sun Court did not technically exist and the protections of the Wyrd on the Freehold frayed.<br /> <br /> During the Edwardian era and through the beginnings of World War I, True Fae interest in the Freehold steadily increased. Vauxhall, in order to preserve his power, refused to name a new Monarch, but also refused to claim royal authority himself so as not to lose face amongst his Entitlement fellows, who valued service as opposed to outright rulership. Everything came to a head in 1917, when Regent Vauxhall's old Keeper brazenly emerged from the Hedge in his full glory, and dragged the poor old Manikin back into Arcadia kicking and screaming, all in very public view.<br /> <br /> Thankfully the grumpy, power hungry Manikin left a will naming Robert &quot;Bob&quot; Duncanson as his successor. Duncanson had served as Knight in the Freehold and Fetch-killer for a long time, and this was accepted with a minimum of grumbling. Duncanson assumed the title of King of the Sun, and kept as his secondary title Knight of Sun's Fire, granted to him some years before by Vauxhall acting on behalf of Bishop Baines (somewhat ironic, given that he was <i>not</i> a fire-aspected Elemental). Following the Freehold's tradition amongst the Sun monarchs of being mostly known by secondary titles, Duncanson was usually referred to simply as &quot;Sir Robert.&quot;<br /> <br /> <i>Known Seemings/Kiths of Historical Figures in this Era:</i><br /> <br /> King Bogumil Sobczak- Darkling Palewraith<br /> <br /> Regent Reeve Vauxhall- Elemental Manikin, Magistrate of the Wax Mask Entitlement<br /> <br /> Sir Robert Duncanson- Elemental Earthbones<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>1918-1950</i></span><br /> <br /> Sir Robert and King Bogumil continued to clash, though not with the intensity they formerly had, during this era. For Sir Robert, it was monstly a matter of age- he had come to the throne already in late middle age. For Sobczak, the death (for all intents and purposes) of his old nemesis Vauxhall seemed to steal the mischevious fire for causing disgust out from under him. The King of the Moon, too, was feeling his age.<br /> <br /> In the late 1940s, after World War II, the current Moon Monarch moved into the area. Supported by the foreign resources of the Phantom Tong, Secret Suzan quickly established herself as a power in the area. Originally courtless, she swore fealty to the Moon Court shortly before the death of Sobczak in 1949. King Bogumil's chosen successor, Tomasz Niezychowski, abdicated less than a year later in favor of Secret Suzan. To this day no one knows exactly what dirt Secret Suzan had on Niezychowski that could cause a Moon courtier enough embarrassment to give up a royal title, but the former King of the Moon returned to his native Poland in 1957 and contact was lost with him. Suzan became the &quot;Dual Queen&quot;, taking the traditional title Queen of the Moon and creating for herself the title Queen of Eclipses.<br /> <br /> Suzan was much more interested in causing disgust in the righteous by stimulating people's vices, and a grand expansion of Moon Court involvement in prostitution, human trafficking, and the nascent drug trade occurred. Sobczak and his ilk prefered to shock and disgust people with their overt displays of paganism and blasphemy (of Christianity only, of course. They were deadly serious about their own faith in Chernobog and Veles). Suzan was nothing if not magnanimous, however, and Sobczak's inner circle eventually came around because she left them to their own devices.<br /> <br /> <i>Known Seemings/Kiths of Historical Figures in this Era:</i><br /> <br /> Queen Suzan- Fairest Shadowsoul, Phantom Tong Entitlement<br /> <br /> King Tomasz Niezychowski- Wizened Miner<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>1950-1997</i></span><br /> <br /> In the early 1960s, Sir Robert's age began to show- he became slightly senile, and concerned about putting his earthly affairs in order. Perhaps providentially, a very unusal Changeling emerged from the hedge. Maurikios, claiming to be priest abducted from Constantinople in the early 1400s, entered the Freehold on August 27th in Elizabeth, New Jersey in 1962. Though his claimes of being of great age were not (and cannot be) verified, the ancient priest was completely mistified by modern technology and was almost immediately killed by cars (which he thought were some kind of fae demons come to reclaim him for his keeper).<br /> <br /> Thankfully Maurikios was discovered by local Blackbird Bishops loyal to the Sun Court, which allowed him to survive. Maurikios's skill at spiritual direction (and the Omen contract) soon earned him a place in Sir Robert's inner circle; the old Episcopalian even converted to Greek Orthodoxy after long discussions with the elderly priest from another age.<br /> <br /> When Sir Robert died in 1970, he indicated that his beloved confessor and spiritual guide, Maurikios, was to take up the reigns of the Sun Court. Maurikios assumed the title King of the Sun, and became the Metropolitan of All Fae. Many outside of Sir Robert's immediate circle of friends were suspicious of Maurikios as some kind of fraud, even a mole put in place by Secret Suzan or one of the True Fae, but their fears were unfounded. By the early 80s, most everyone came to love their anachronistic old Metropolitan and his medieval sensibilites, not in the least because he was very willing to meet with and council all Changelings in the area, even Moon courtiers.<br /> <br /> Things were not to last, however. As time passed, Maurikios's Byzantine (literally) view of morality began to clash with many younger memebers of the Sun Court. These younger courtiers were raised on the more liberal morality of late 20th century America, and began to feel that it was Maurikios and his supporters who should feel shame for their antiquated and &quot;bigoted&quot; opinions- not alleged sinners.<br /> <br /> A faction began to grow in the Sun Court known derisively, but then formally, as the Modernist Sun. The faction started out small, but began to grow over the years. The Modernists also had far more contacts with the human world, and were able to make use of the growing power of anti-discrimnation laws, powerful identity interest groups, and other facets of contemporary politics to force their ways on Maurikios's faction (which became known, eventually, as the Orthodox Sun).<br /> <br /> Things came to a head in 1997, when the unofficial leader of the Modernist Sun, a Changeling named Chris Grand, publicaly challenged Maurikios for the title of King of the Sun on Kupala Day. Maurikios refused, calling Grand a unrepentant sodomite, and a man who makes virtues out of vices and vices out of virtues. The supporters of each candidate quickly began hurling accusations and invective at each other, attempting to shame the other group away from power. The event even turned into a small riot that had to be broken up by police, something which had to be assiduously covered up by Secret Suzan in order to protect the hidden existence of the fae. This event became known as the Great Schism, and it precipitated a brief civil war in the Sun Court.<br /> <br /> <i>Known Seemings/Kiths of Historical Figures in this Era:</i><br /> <br /> Metropolitan Maurikios- Wizened Author<br /> <br /> Chris Grand- Ogre Cyclopean<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>1997- Present</i></span><br /> <br /> The Great Schism provoked violence between the two factions of the Sun Court. In addition, the dispute over who was King of the Sun caused the title to, in the eyes of the Wyrd, be defunct. Without the balance of Sun and Moon, the eyes of the Gentry once again began to become focused on the Freehold of the Palisades.<br /> <br /> The first person to realize this was Secret Suzan. Using her extensive network of spies and criminals, she quickly brokered a truce between the two factions of the Sun Court that exists to this day. Each year, on Kupala Day, power would pass between Chris Grand and Maurikios. When he is in power, Maurikios would be known as King of the Sun and Metropolitan of All Fae. When Chris Grand is in power, he would be known as King of the Sun and First Citizen of the Freehold. In years when they were not power, the other would relinquish all titles, and become a commoner.<br /> <br /> This truce in the Great Schism remains in force today, with a careful balance of power between Maurikios and Grand, enforced by Secret Suzan (secretly, of course). Power is due to pass from Maurikios to Grand on Kupala Day (in the Freehold, June 23-24) of 2015.<br /> <br /> ----<br /> My next post will likely be information about and important personages in the modern Courts of the Freehold.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-01-2015, 05:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-01T17:08:41-08:00","id":357071,"title":"","content":"Looks interesting. I've always found the Sun and Moon Courts really interesting, but their lack of Contracts and their rivalry (a two court system where the two courts don't get along) does not allow for the variety of PC options I prefer. Of course, in MDH this was why I had them in control of LA proper, so they would be an option and their machinations not too big deal. The section says they often share motleys, but it seems hairy, though maybe I have just not seen it done well. Of course, I have not seen it done poorly, either; all four local Changeling games, including my two, have been in Seasonal Freeholds. (though BTS had an area controlled by Directional, and of course MDH has LA as Dinural and Chinatown as Directional). I look forward to seeing how it works for you, though.<br /> <br /> You have some excellent work on history there, I like it a lot. Honestly it very much exceeds the history in my game (Malibu/Santa Monica/Venice Beach changeling history can be summed up in about two paragraphs mostly pertaining to Golden Swan, while LA proper and Hollywood have a bit more since it shaped the area a bit). I think there is some interesting stuff there that can feed the modern game, so bravo. A lot of the historical personalities are interesting. If nothing else, it is always fun to play with non-Cyrillic Eastern European lettering systems; Polish has some interesting names with all the dialectic marks. If they scare your players, remind them their purpose is just to make the vowels and such non-guesswork for pronunciation. If they are arcane, just sound it out as if they are not there. You get some oddities (\u0160\u00e1rka is more like Shark-ah than Ssark-ah) but it is still doable. If you are unsure, <a href=\"http://www.forvo.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Forvo.com</a> is your friend, and an easy way to get name pronunciations.<br /> <br /> My only issue would be the Phantom Tong is supposed to be a destabilizing force in a Freehold, while Secret Suzan is not only not actively destabilizing but actually important to keeping it whole. If you want to keep her PT I'd recommend maybe having that as an angle for some issue within the Entitlement surrounding her. Also, I would be remiss in not noting I like the name &quot;Secret Suzan.&quot;<br /> <br /> Look forward to seeing more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-01T21:16:33-08:00","id":357180,"title":"","content":"Thanks Baron, your comments mean a lot to me. I will keep in mind what you say about Secret Suzan. If my players decide to become more involved with her (I intend to run the game somewhat sandboxy), I will have to flesh it out. At the moment I am leaning toward her being on the &quot;corrupt&quot; side of the Tong, out just for money and power in the underworld, but rationalizing her decisions as being in line with Tong ideology since keeping the Sun Court divided under truce keeps the Freehold more unstable than if they had a single unified leader.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-01T21:36:03-08:00","id":357186,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Court of the Sun</b></span><br /> <br /> Foundation Date: June 23-34, 1777<br /> <br /> Rulership over the Freehold: Variable; from Sun-up to Sun-down every day.<br /> <br /> Ruler: The King of the Sun, a title held either by Maurikios, or Christopher Grand, and switiching between the two anually on Kupala Day.<br /> <br /> <i>Common Practices</i><br /> <br /> Lucky Sevens: Both the Moon Court and the Sun Court consider the number 7 to be sacred to the whole Freehold. The seven founders of the Freehold in 1777 likely contributed to this, but important things tend to happen in the Freehold in years including the number 7, or on days and years that are a multiple of the number seven. Rare is the Changeling, even a courtless, who does not have some king of stylized representation of the number 7 stashed somewhere in his house.<br /> <br /> Kupala Night (&amp; Day): The Sun Court in the Freehold of the Palisades observes this day according to Polish custom, on the 23-24 of June. The traditional activities of this day have been deformed somewhat by the Great Schism truce, which stipulates that power is to pass from the leader of the Modernist faction to the leader of the Orthodox faction on this day, and back again the next year. Therefore the focus of the day is on the coronation of the new King of the Sun, though the coronation ceremony is usually followed by traditional Kupala day activities. Especially in the case of years that the Orthodox faction takes power, the coronation is followed by a large number of baptisms.<br /> <br /> Bishop Baines Memorial: On July 7th, his birthday, the Sun Court commemorates the memory of the most beloved Sun monarch to reign in the Freehold, Bishop Gerald Baines. Baines is something of a combination between a folk hero and a saint for Sun courtiers, in large part because a painting of his Mask survives from his time as vicar in the little Episcopalain parish of Philipsburg. It is considered gauche for Sun Courtiers to not have a copy or print of this portrait somewhere in their possession, either hanging in their house or in a small miniature kept in a medallion locket. More religious Sun courtiers will typically attend or have a religious ritual said in his honor on this day. More secular types will simply raise a glass in his memory at dinner. It is traditional for Sun Courtiers of sufficient means to give gifts of sherry, the Bishop's beverage of choice, on this day to any Changeling who may ask for a bottle. Needless to say, a large number of Moon Court drunks love this holiday too.<br /> <br /> <i>Factions</i><br /> <br /> The Sun court is split by a very bitter divide between the Modernist and Orthodox factions. Typically, people of a more secular bent tend to align with the Modernist faction, while Christians tend to align with the Orthodox faction, though this is not an iron rule. People of neither ideological persuasion tend to fall into the orbit of one of the other faction through personal connections and experiences. For example, a Muslim Sun courtier who is on the recieving end of one of Maurikios's legendary rants against the perfidy of &quot;Mohammedans&quot; might be pushed into alignment with the Modernist faction, while another who thinks women ought to remain covered out of modesty might be driven into the Orthodox faction when Modernist Sun courtiers send his boss screenshots of his &quot;misogynistic&quot; message board postings, and he finds himself out of a job.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking, the Modernist faction is numerically superior, wealthier in human money, and is more well connected to local human power structures, especially the New Jersey state government, media, and academia.<br /> <br /> The Orthodox faction tends to have more experienced (though not necessarily older) Changelings who have a deeper understanding of the Wyrd, more Contracts with arcane forces, and even contacts with other supernatural creatures. The Orthodox faction boasts the great majority of Changelings with an Entitlement in the Sun Court.<br /> <br /> As is common with the Sun Court, both sides favor high Clarity and it is not uncommon for Sun Courtiers to maintain Clarity at 7 or even 8 dots (though of course they would not describe it this way).<br /> <br /> <i>The King of the Sun</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Maurikios<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Fr. Maurikios &quot;Maurice&quot; Lenardos<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Maurikios<br /> <br /> Titles: In years when he is reigning over the Sun Court, Maurikios claims the titles King of the Sun and Metropolitan of All Fae. In years he is not reigning, he loses all titles and becomes a commoner<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Mendelssohn's &quot;Yet Doth the Lord&quot;, the fifth movement of the Elijah oratorio: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UzudEVr6gM8\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UzudEVr6gM8</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> Maurikios appears as a Greek Orthodox priest in his mid-late 70's. He has a full flowing white beard, a black robe and kalimavkion with a veil. He has a slightly bent posture and moves slowly, requiring a cane to walk. Though he really is a hieromonk, he is not really a bishop. However, when acting as Metropolitan of All Fae and King of the Sun, Maurikios will wear clothing and vestments appropriate to that station on ceremonial occasions.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> To be honest, Maurikios's mein is not all that different from his Mask. His stoop becomes even more pronounced. The lines and wrinkles in his face become even deeper. His thick hair and beard fade away to just a few diaphonous strands. His fingers grow longer and become stained with ink.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> Maurikios began life as the son of simple cobbler in Constantinople. As a young boy he was considered naturally bright, and he was taught to read and write by his local parish priest. The boy's attentive devotion to sacred things during the Divine liturgy, as well as his excellent penmanship, saw him enter the Chora monastery at the relatively young age of 15.<br /> <br /> Maurikios became known, if not as an original thinker, as very well versed in theology, an excellent confessor and spiritual director, and as an illuminator and transcriber of both sacred texts and mundane philosophical works. His life would have happily passed in quiet prayer and contemplation of Divine things in the Chora if not for one small fact: the Byzantine Empire was tottering and the Emperors were rapidly losing their ability to defend Constantinople.<br /> <br /> The end came in 1453, when the Ottoman Turks sacked, then occupied, the shining jewel of Eastern Christendom. In the chaos and confusion of the sack, Fr. Maurikios tried to flee, running through the winding, narrow streets of the city. Then she appeared to him, a holy vision of the Mother of God. Beckoning him into a dark alleyway, promising hope of salvation from the Mohammedan hordes for her devoted monk, Maurikios could do little but obey her summons. It was a choice he came to regret.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> The vision was not the Virgin Mary, but something altogether more sinister. A terrible True Fae calling herself the False Theotokos, as soon as Maurikios entered her Rancid Cloister, she threw him into chains as her slave.<br /> <br /> The Kindly One took the form of a pregnant Madonna, but when she was ready to &quot;give birth&quot;, her stomach split wide open to reveal a horrible grey womb that disgorged terrible creatures. Sometimes the creatures were fetches, sometimes Hobs, sometimes they were even young True Fae. Other times the False Theotokos opened her womb to stuff a human captive inside, only to disgorge him at a later date as a horribly deformed Changeling.<br /> <br /> Thankfully Maurikios managed to avoid this dire fate, but he was changed all the same. The False Theotokos's abiding love was for blasphemy, which she assiduously wished to spread in the mortal world, and her true hate was for Christ, whose reign in the world she assiduously desired to end. She commanded Maurikios to copy the sacred texts and works of philosophy in order to give them to her profane children, that they might be better prepared to go out into the world to spread error. Eventually she had Maurikios not only copy these texts, but tutor and debate with her foul progeny in order to hone their skill in attacking and denuding sacred things.<br /> <br /> Maurikios could not resist. If he refused to write, he would be whipped, then starved for days. Eventually the False Theotokos would just snap her fingers, and he would write anyway under magical compulsion. He tried to remain silent when forced to give lessons on sacred things to decidedly devilish creatures, but he would receive the same treatment, and then forced by magic to debate anyway. Maurikios resisted bravely for the first decade. After that... he put his faith in God to deliver him, and just did as the False Theotokos bid him.<br /> <br /> Maurikios could not say how long his Durance truly was. Sometimes, it felt like 100 years, sometimes 1000, sometimes just a few months. Maurikios does not know what he said or did, but he does know that he convinced the False Theotokos that she did not need an old monk like him any more. The Kindly One agreed, and had one of her Ogre servants/children grab Maurikios by the leg and heave him out of the Rancid Cloister. Maurikios remembers falling, being hit repeatedly by branches and torn by thorns on the way down, but nothing more specific about the journey. He tumbled out of the Hedge in Elizabeth, NJ, in the early 1960s.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Maurikios was dazed and confused, at first, by the modern world. Most immediately, he was struck with amazement that there was another continent, another two continents, out beyond Gibraltar, and humans had reached them not long after he had been taken.<br /> <br /> He couldn't understand how he had found his way to this time and place so distant from his own, but he finally understood a few days after he emerged from the Hedge. Handled carefully by Blackbird Bishops, the Entitlement quickly developed a simple false identity for the elderly monk as a retired priest who would be taking up residence in St Fanourios church, just a few block from where he emerged. After the deception was in place, the human pastor of the church showed Maurikios around the parish facilities. The parish's greatest possession, he said, was a real 15th century illuminated Bible gifted to them by the Ecumenical Patriarch some years ago.<br /> <br /> The pastor opened the old wooden case, and staring up at Maurikios was a Bible that he had personally transcribed and illuminated 600 years ago. Maurikios knew then that it was not just Fae caprice that had him show up in this place, but true Providence.<br /> <br /> The other relevant parts of Maurikios's history are covered in the general history of the Freehold, above<br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> After the initial wonder of the new world had worn off, Maurikios realized a terrible truth: the world had many gadgets, but had forsaken God. The Church itself was torn into a million million competing sects, many of which he could barely recognize as being Christian. And then he realized: some of this was certainly his own fault. By teaching and writing for the False Theotokos and her blasphemous children, he gave them the tools they needed to pick apart glorious Christendom and replace it with American Bandstand, snake-handlers, and psychedelic drugs. The cold grip of shame on his heart for his complicity in this resolved itself into a fiery wrath at those who would call evil good, and good evil. If he had had a hand, however small, in causing the current state of affairs, it was clear that God wanted him to have a hand in undoing it.<br /> <br /> Maurikios is kind and grandfatherly to everyone he meets. As long as he is approached humbly, and by people with a repentant heart, there is nothing that the old hieromonk will not do for them. His favorite quotation from the bible is, after all, &quot;Who then shall separate us from the love of Christ? Shall tribulation? or distress? or famine? or nakedness? or danger? or persecution? or the sword?&quot; He knows the truth of this quotation better than most. Maurikios is very adept at identifying people's vices, and what is underlying those vices, and advising them on how best to resist them.<br /> <br /> Conversely, Maurikios has no time for public, unrepentant sinners or those who make an ideology out of sin. It is very well that he is both old, and relatively frail and inneffective in combat, because he would certainly be moved to personal violence more often than not against those who sin against the Holy Spirit. He knows it is inappropriate for a monk to become so angry, so he tries to have subordinates in the Sun Court deal with Modernist Sun courtiers and the more egregious offenders amongst the Moon courtiers. He doesn't want to cleanse the world of sin, really, which he knows is an impossible task, but merely wants sinners to feel shame for their erroneous ways, and then repent, as they should.<br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231cEQzUm1IV3hFTGs/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>Maurikios suspects, but has no direct proof, that Secret Suzan favors his faction in the Great Schism (and she does, see below). This is a source of some concern for him, but he doesn't really know what to do about it. The Court Goodwill (Moon) merit is intended to reflect this favor by the Queen of Eclipses.<br /> <br /> Maurikios relies on his Animation contract to deal with modern technology. Unfortunately he is only willing to use it around other Changelings, and otherwise has to rely on them for help operating electronics when regular humans are around. He still does not know how to drive, and is in fact slightly frightened by automobiles, trains, and planes.<br /> <br /> ----<br /> <br /> First Citizen Chris Grand will be posted tomorrow evening (probably).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-05-2015, 12:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T07:35:19-08:00","id":357456,"title":"","content":"Certainly an interesting setting. Does the Freehold have any sort of contact with others nearby? Have non-diurnal Courts ever attempted to establish a foothold? What sort of Supernatural contacts do the Orthodox sun have? Sorry if this is irritating you!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T16:20:48-08:00","id":357881,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play?p=357456#post357456\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Certainly an interesting setting. Does the Freehold have any sort of contact with others nearby? Have non-diurnal Courts ever attempted to establish a foothold? What sort of Supernatural contacts do the Orthodox sun have? Sorry if this is irritating you!</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> No problem at all Zealous! I was hoping for questions like yours in order to help me refine the setting. As far as nearby Freeholds go, I do have an idea of what they are. I was planning on making a post on them with a small description of each a bit later on.<br /> <br /> My general thought is that after Colonel Wei\u00dfekopf and the six other founders established the Freehold, there were not any major attempts by Seasonal courts to compete with him. Part of the reason is that all of the surrounding Freeholds were on the Seasonal model, so there was not really a big &quot;threat&quot; from one oddity in the region. Having a Moon Court nearby is also useful as dumping ground for troublemakers.<br /> <br /> I actually haven't given much thought to what sort of other supernaturals might have contacts with the Orthodox Sun courtiers. My guess is that, since the Orthodox Sun is more inclined toward pursuing &quot;mystical&quot; goals, they might have some light contact with Mages (especially Acanthus, for those interested in the Wyrd, and Obrimos, who have an intersecting interest in religion). They might also have a vague idea that there is a group of vampires who participate in a kind of heinous parody of Christianity, but I also think that both Modernist and Orthodox Sun courtiers would consider vampires &quot;Moon court business&quot; for obvious reasons.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-02-2015, 08:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T17:28:16-08:00","id":357912,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Formosus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play?p=357180#post357180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks Baron, your comments mean a lot to me. I will keep in mind what you say about Secret Suzan. If my players decide to become more involved with her (I intend to run the game somewhat sandboxy), I will have to flesh it out. At the moment I am leaning toward her being on the &quot;corrupt&quot; side of the Tong, out just for money and power in the underworld, but rationalizing her decisions as being in line with Tong ideology since keeping the Sun Court divided under truce keeps the Freehold more unstable than if they had a single unified leader.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is cool. Let me stress (again) I am not saying &quot;you are doing it wrong&quot; just looking at how that stabilizing aspect intersects with the Phantom Tong as I understand it. Quite frankly, If you like her as you wrote her and you decide it does go against the PT, either make that a plot point or change the PT in your campaign. Just tell players that who read about the PT if you do.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\">Forum Rules</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\">This is my mod voice.</span> This is my goth voice.<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/geist-the-sin-eaters/15731-geist-balance-of-shadows-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">[Geist: Balance of Shadows</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/17537-v20-the-conspiracy-of-hrad-%C4%8Dern%C3%A1-hora-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Vampire: The Conspiracy of Hrad \u010cern\u00e1 Hora</a><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/13838-scion-bohemian-front-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\"> ][ Scion: Bohemian Front</a>][<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/99033-changeling-malibu-dream-house-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Changeling: Malibu Dream House</a>] [<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/268069-demon-night-train-detective-agency-actual-play\" target=\"_blank\">Demon: Night Train Detective Agency</a>] <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/la94fvk\" target=\"_blank\">[WoD: The Golden Eagle</a>]","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/56-baroness-nerak","name":"Baroness Nerak","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar56_2.jpeg","title":"Moderator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2524"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-02T20:12:21-08:00","id":357984,"title":"","content":"<i>The King of the Sun</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Lukasz &quot;Luke&quot; Nowak<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Christopher &quot;Chris&quot; Grand<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Christopher &quot;Chris&quot; Grand<br /> <br /> Titles: In years when he is reigning over the Sun Court, Grand claims the titles King of the Sun and First Citizen of the Freehold. In years he is not reigning, he loses all titles and becomes a commoner.<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Pet Shop Boys - It's a Sin <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J-4gAetwNkw\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J-4gAetwNkw</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> &quot;Large&quot; is probably the first word that comes to mind when viewing Chris Grand. Topping off at just under 7 feet tall, hugely muscular, and possessing slightly over-sized, powerful hands, the leader of the Modernist faction is an imposing sight. Neither particularly ugly nor particularly handsome, Grand has blue eyes and broad, round features indicative of an Eastern European heritage. His light blond hair is mostly gone from the top of his head, leaving a corona around the sides (though none of it has gone white yet). Grand dresses appropriately for the occasion, and his wealth allows him to purchase whatever he needs to either blend in or stand out.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> The largest difference between Grand's Mask and Mien is that his regular &quot;human&quot; eyes are gone and the sockets covered over with flesh. They are replaced by a large, watery, lidless gray-blue eye in the center of his forehead. Other than this most startling change, Grand also looks a bit younger and more vital in his Mien- for one, his baldness is gone- replaced with a full head of blond hair.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> The son of 2nd generation Polish immigrants to the United States, Lukasz Nowak's young life in Secaucus was one of quiet working class desperation. It's not that his parent's were cruel or unkind, far from it. It's just that everything was <i>gray. </i>His father's construction job was gray concrete and gray overalls. His mother's home-patched clothes were gray. The bland canned foods they ate were gray, the habit of the nuns who taught him at the Catholic school he could barely afford to go to were gray. Even their tiny house overlooking the Hackensack River (itself an unpleasant gray color) was all grays and neutral colors. Lukasz always wanted something more, something colorful. He felt alienated from the hum-drum life around him of paychecks on Friday, drinks on Saturday, Mass on Sunday, and back to work on Monday.<br /> <br /> Unlike many people who don't feel like they belong, Luke was never troubled or bullied. That's because he was big. Really big, like his father before him. He was almost 6 feet tall by the time he was 12 years old. By that age, his father had already clandestinely conscripted him into helping with his construction work. Luke began to see whatever colorful future he envisioned slipping away as he was sucked into the ins and outs of work-a-day life.<br /> <br /> All of that changed shortly after his 13th birthday, in 1970. He doesn't remember exactly what he did, but he knows he cursed his father out for the first time over something related to construction work. His father gave him a smack (and was just about the only person large enough to do so without any repercussions) and sent him to bed without dinner. He might have prayed to God, or to anyone who would hear him, to take him away from his gray life. When he woke up in the morning, he wasn't in New Jersey any more.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> The Limestone Governess grew rock formations and stalactites in her cavern. It sounds boring, but it would be impossible to do justice to the beauty of what she made. Fantastic natural sculptures that defied reason painted in colors that could not be described were the least of the rocky wonders in her little underground corner of Arcadia. To protect these marvelous formations, the Limestone Governess needed guardians- specifically Cyclopean Changelings who could spot any intruder from the outside that might ruin her creations, and any defect in the formations themselves that needed to be corrected.<br /> <br /> And so Lukasz Nowak was transformed into one of these Cyclopeans and set to guard the Limestone Governess's works. Luke finally had the color he had always wanted.<br /> <br /> Guard duty was not the only reason the Governess liked Cyclopeans and other Ogres though. She was a giantess, and she had certain needs that could only be fulfilled by what some might call &quot;big boys.&quot; Sometimes she laid with her &quot;boys&quot; herself, sometimes, she would have them lie with each other as she watched, admiring their bodies. The first time Luke was taken into her bedchamber, he was afraid. The 50th time, he was resigned. The 100th time, he started to enjoy it. Not everyone did though, and the resentment grew. One of the other Cyclopeans, Grand cannot remember his name, staged a breakout. He shattered one of the Limestone Governess's precious stalagmites in a spray of rainbows and crystal stone, sending their keeper into paroxysm of rage. In her delirious state, many of her Ogres ran for it. Truth be told, Grand was almost not one of the them, but as he heard the bellows of rage from the Governess, he realized that he didn't need her any more. He could bring color to the <i>real</i> world, even to that little gray town on the Hackensack River that floated hazily in his memory. He ran with other Ogres, squirming through tight tunnels of sharp stones and crystals that tore at his flesh and soul in equal parts. He emerged in a well-hidden alley, nearly naked, not far from his father's favorite watering hole. The year was 1980.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Once he had gotten his bearings in the real world, Grand wasted no time in living the life that <i>he </i>wanted to live. He quickly changed his name to something he felt more reflected what was on his inside. He got into competitive bodybuilding, which he was very successful at, and which he eventually parlayed into small roles in film and television. He spent his weekends at colorful, bright bars with beautiful people and loud music, not the dark, smoky places his father had always preferred. He checked up on his fetch and pretty much decided the thing could have his old life- it had married the plain girl from next door right out of high school, and was now busy working at the same construction work as his father and grandfather, living in the same type of small house with the same type of small ambitions.<br /> <br /> Chris Grand remained courtless for a long time. Besides occasionally hopping into the city or out to Los Angeles for a role, and traveling around the country for bodybuilding contests, neither the Sun nor the Moon court much captured his attention. By 1985 he even considered relocating to Hollywood permanently and joining up with the Spring Court there. It was then that Grand became cognizant of the AIDS scourge.<br /> <br /> Suddenly many of his human longtime friends, and even lovers, began to sicken and die. Then the Changelings started to die too. The news said it was sickness from Africa, the preachers said it was a plague from God. Grand came to believe that it was only his own turbo-charged Ogrish constitution that had saved him. He thought briefly of joining the Moon court, but then he realized that Disgust would not help him. Disgust is the power of the outsider. What he needed was the power of the insider- what he needed was <i>shame, </i>to create a new morality that would make sure no one like him could be ignored or dismissed as sinner or freak. It would be the people who did that who would be considered sinners and freaks.<br /> <br /> He was put in contact with a small group of dissident Sun courtiers in 1988, mostly from mainline Protestant churches, who agreed with him, and they accepted him into the fold. By 1992, Grand's charisma, media connections, and sheer size saw him rocket to the forefront of the so-called Modernist Sun faction. As he got older, he stopped his competitive bodybuilding and toned down his appearances in entertainment media and began to focus more on advocacy.<br /> <br /> Grand ultimately got what he wanted, being a key player in huge shift in Western societal mores in the waning of the 20th century. He was unable to permanently unseat his main rival, the hieromonk Maurikios, but that was only a secondary goal anyway. After his quasi- ascension as First Citizen of the Freehold, Grand founded the Citizen Concerned for Equality lobby group just across the street from the state capitol building in Trenton (though he lives in a pleasant estate in much nicer Princeton). From there, he coordinates both his campaigns in both the human and Changeling world.<br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> It is axiomatic that if you want to beat the Man, you have to become the Man. Many people of Chris Grand's political persuasion like to pretend, even to themselves, that they are paragons of tolerance and inclusion. Grand is happy to put up a similar pretense in public, but does not have any such illusions about what he really has become; he is in the business of shame, after all. Take, for example, his &quot;charity&quot; Citizens Concerned for Equality. It is ostensibly suppose to monitor and report on corruption and &quot;extreme&quot; politics in New Jersey. In reality, it exists to hound and shame anyone who may have once uttered a right-wing thought, especially of a &quot;social&quot; variety, out of public life. Grand makes the talk show circuit around the New York metropolitan area, breaking out the &quot;how could you say thats&quot; and sad stories about gay Eagle Scouts and just soaks up all of the shame it produces.<br /> <br /> Despite all the power he wields in the human world, though, he is constantly troubled by his inability to break the Orthodox Sun like he has so many others. It would be wrong to say he's obsessed with a complete take over of the Sun Court, but his hatred for these recalcitrant throwbacks far outstrips anything he feels for the Moon Court. In fact, his only concern for the Moon Court is trying to figure out how to get them to drop their support for the Great Schism Truce, even if they don't actually back him. He's certain that without the Moon behind the truce, the full force of modernity will fall on the Orthodox Sun like a ton of bricks.<br /> <br /> Life is not all politics, however. Deep inside, the 13 year old Luke Nowak, who just wanted a brighter, more colorful life is hiding. The 23 year old Cyclopean who went to fun parties and danced with beautiful people is in there, too. They come out in brief glimpses, on Kupala Day, at birthdays of close friends, sometimes when he's alone at home and puts on his old 80's pop music albums. Someone who can reach those two might be surprised at how big Chris Grand can really be.<br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: </b><a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231YVMtclZmUTFiM2c/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>Grand was never formally trained in a martial art (represented by his lack of a style), but his bit of work in film (usually as a thug or villain) combined with his experience as a guard in Arcadia (where the Limestone Governess just figured the size of her servants was enough training and they would just pummel intruders into submission- which basically turned out to be true) are the source of his skill in brawl.<br /> <br /> I didn't really go into this in the background, but like many young men flush with new money, the post-Durance Chris Grand developed a taste for fast cars (which he retains to this day). Hence the high drive skill.<br /> <br /> I think everything else is fairly self explanatory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-03-2015, 07:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T16:18:05-08:00","id":358595,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Court of the Moon</b></span><br /> <br /> Foundation Date: December 24, 1817 (Winter Solstice/Korochun)<br /> <br /> Rulership over the Freehold: Variable; from Sun-down to Sun-up every day.<br /> <br /> Ruler: The King of the Moon, held by &quot;Secret&quot; Suzan (as Queen of the Moon).<br /> <br /> Thanks to the Great Schism, the Moon Court is comfortably the more powerful of the two Courts in the Freehold of the Palisades. At one point, the Sun Court as whole outnumbered the Moon Court, but the Great Schism is now going on 20 years, which has resulted in the Moon Court attracting more members who wanted to avoid the terrible sectarian strife of the Court of the Sun. Consequently, the Moon Court now also slightly outnumbers the Sun Court.<br /> <br /> <i>Common Practices</i><br /> <br /> Lucky Sevens: Both the Moon Court and the Sun Court consider the number 7 to be sacred to the whole Freehold. The seven founders of the Freehold in 1777 likely contributed to this, but important things tend to happen in the Freehold in years including the number 7, or on days and years that are a multiple of the number seven. Rare is the Changeling, even a courtless, who does not have some king of stylized representation of the number 7 stashed somewhere in his house.<br /> <br /> Korochun: This variable feast takes place on the Winter Solstice every year. The night is given over mostly to revelry and the playing of pranks on Sun Courtiers (usually of a harmless variety). Chernobogists (see below) take part in the revelry, of course, but this is also a sacred day for them. Rumors circulate every year that they take part in &quot;pranks&quot; of a significantly darker nature than other Moon Courtiers on this night in order to please their god.<br /> <br /> Washington's Birthday: Many of the original founders of the Sun Court had loyalist (of the British, not the Fae, kind) sympathies during the American Revolution. When the Moon Court was founded, Washington's Birthday (February 22nd) was semi-officially adopted as Korochun 2.0 for the purpose of annoying and disgusting Sun Court members. As time passed and the original political meaning of the holiday wore away, the holiday lost this flavor and instead became a general revelry/thanksgiving celebration for Moon courtiers. With the advent of &quot;Secret&quot; Suzan's reign and her connections to the criminal world, it became common for Moon Courtiers to use this day for vulgar displays of wealth, especially if the wealth was gotten via illicit means. It should be noted that Changelings in the Freehold always celebrate Washington's Birthday on his <i>actual</i> birthday, February 22nd, not on the official day set by the Federal government.<br /> <br /> <i>Factions</i><br /> <br /> The Moon Court's internal factions do not hold even a tenth as much rancor for each other as the Sun Court's internal factions do. That said, relations are not always rosy.<br /> <br /> <b>Bogumilists/Chernobogists</b>: This faction consists of people who were closely tied to the reigns of Bogumil Sobczak and Tomasz Niezychowski The &quot;outer party&quot;, so to speak, primarily consists of people of Eastern European extraction, and are known as Bogumilists. These people tend to have strong connections to the old country or are recent immigrants- a person of Lithuanian descent whose family has been in the United States for 5 generations and who is fully assimilated into American culture is far more likely to be unaligned than to be a Bogumilist. Bogumilists tend to be Poles above all else, and this sometimes leads to Russians, in particular, not being welcome in the faction (though some sneak through anyway). After unaligned Moon Courtiers, Bogumilists are probably the largest faction.<br /> <br /> Chernobogists are the &quot;inner party&quot; of the Bogumilist faction. They are almost exclusively Poles and they make their dislike of Russians (and Germans, should they find any), fairly plain. Most importantly, Chernobogists are neo-pagans who follow an ersatz religion cooked up by old King Bogumil based on ancient Slavic mythology and, if they are to be believed, ancient truths he learned during his Durance. They take Chernobog to be their patron deity with a lesser emphasis on Veles as a secondary god. Chernobogism is not something you will find on the shelves of the New Age rack of the local chain bookstore; it is made of decidedly darker, sterner stuff. Consequently, Chernobogists tend to live on the fringes of human society, and many actually dwell in the Hedge itself. Without exception they are experienced occultists and tend to be deep into contracts with darker things (Darkness, Moon, and Lucidity being favorites amongst them).<br /> <br /> Chernobogists &amp; Bogumilists made their peace with Queen Suzan long ago and do not actively oppose her reign, but nor do they particularly care for her meddling in their affairs. Suzan has thus far kept up the appearance of leaving them to their own devices (which in truth, she mostly does), but she is a master of deception and it is unlikely they would ever notice if she did decide to interfere.<br /> <br /> <b>Phantom Tong</b>: This factions ties itself most closely to &quot;Secret&quot; Suzan. The name of the faction derives from Queen Suzan and her inner circle, who are all actually members of the Phantom Tong Entitlement. Most of the people who would be considered part of the faction are merely aspiring to the Entitlement (at best), or are poseurs (and sometimes sycophants) at worst. With few exceptions Phantom Tong faction members are involved in criminal activities, usually centered around what Police departments used to call &quot;vice&quot;- drug production, shipment, and sales, and prostitution/human trafficking. Some of the less successful Changelings considered members are simply petty thieves or muggers, while some of the more successful have never seen a dark alley or whorehouse because they are million dollar embezzlers or tax cheats. The Phantom Tong is probably the third largest faction, though it is by far the wealthiest.<br /> <br /> <b>Scorpion Legitimists</b>: The smallest faction (there are perhaps 12-14 Changelings considered members), Scorpion Legitimists are Moon courtiers who believe that all of the Moon Monarchs since Zebulon Weeks have been illegitimate, because Weeks was overthrown in a coup. Obviously, these events happened long ago and most Moon courtiers consider them hardly relevant today, however, Scorpion Legitimists have always been assiduous about &quot;adopting&quot; a<span style=\"color:#000000\"> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">prot\u00e9g\u00e9 and passing on their political beliefs. Scorpion Legitimists tend to be eccentric, even by Moon Courtier standards, though in relatively harmless ways (for example, one is an enormous proponent of the crackpot Time Cube theory). The faction is not to be trifled with, however, because its members tend to inherit the fortunes of their mentors, including mundane money, large and well protected Hollows, and powerful (4-5 dot) tokens. Despite their seemingly harmless eccentricity, Scorpion Legitimists are often personally powerful as well- they refuse to pledge fealty to the Freehold's Moon monarch, and instead undertake the arduous quests necessary to make individual pacts with the Moon itself. Scorpion Legitimists enjoy better relations with the Sun Court than any other faction of Moon Courtiers, and a number of the more powerful ones are on friendly, speaking terms with Metropolitan Maurikios who has a great deal of sympathy for their cause.<br /> <br /> <b>Unaligned</b>: A simple majority of Moon courtiers in the Freehold do not have strong ties to any particular political faction. They generally are loyal to Queen Suzan but do not make a great show of it, nor do they attempt to emulate her like the Phantom Tong aspirants do. Otherwise, it is very hard to categorize them.</span></span><br /> <br /> Next up: Queen Suzan and her second, Salacious L (possibly later tonight, otherwise tomorrow).</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-04-2015, 06:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T19:28:40-08:00","id":358743,"title":"","content":"&quot;Bogumilists/Chernobogists&quot; ... They do realize he actually never existed, right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-03T20:03:30-08:00","id":358765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play?p=358743#post358743\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Bogumilists/Chernobogists&quot; ... They do realize he actually never existed, right?</div> </div> </div> </div>*waves hands in a distracting manner* Ancient truths learned in Arcadia my friend, ancient truths.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T11:59:55-08:00","id":359363,"title":"","content":"I'm not entirely please with Suzan and I feel like it doesn't come together all that well. Suggestions are welcome (as always).<br /> <br /> <i>The Queen of the Moon</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Susan Goldner<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Secret Suzan does not really have close relationships with mere humans, and so is not well known enough amongst them to have a consistent name. If she does have to deal with them in her real Mask, she simply gives a fake name that she changes every time.<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Queen Suzan, Secret Suzan (both are in equal usage)<br /> <br /> Titles: Queen of the Moon, Queen of Eclipses, Dai Lo of the Phantom Tong<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Fleetwood Mac - Rihannon: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mDkKyBU7GCs\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mDkKyBU7GCs</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> On the rare occasion when Secret Suzan must show her &quot;real&quot; Mask to the world, she appears as a petite woman in her mid-late 60's with dusky skin, dark eyes, and graying hair. She prefers clothing that is in somber colors and that has severe, pointy angles. She also has a fondness for hats that incorporate a partial veil. Conversely, she will sometimes wear her hair down and dress in loose, flowing dresses and skirts that are reminiscent of smoke or fog. Suzan has a voice that, to many Americans, has a generic &quot;Continental European&quot; accent that can't quite be placed.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> Suzan's mien looks exactly like her Mask, except <i>everything </i>is pitch, matte black. Her hair, skin, eyes, fingernails- everything is perfectly black. If she's cut, even her blood is the same black color. Thanks to being a Phantom Tong member, no matter what form she's wearing, in cold weather dessicated, skeletal faces can be seen in her steaming breath.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> The Goldners of D\u00fcsseldorf were a well established family in the international diamond trade, with connections ranging all over the world, from Africa to the Far East. Little Susan's early childhood was idyllic and carefree, her weeks spent taking lessons at home from her mother, and weekends spent going to see her dear old Great Uncle, who was the Rabbi at the synagogue just down the street. Susan just couldn't understand why her parents seemed to grow more and more worried as the years passed; they wouldn't tell her why, just brushing it off, if she asked. Her world was turned upside down in 1939, when her parents relocated the whole family to Hong Kong (where a great deal of her father's diamond business was) for reasons she could barely understand.<br /> <br /> Susan hated the new city; the people were strange, they spoke a strange language, and none of her friends were there. Worst of all, however, was her terrible new nanny. Nanny Caixia barely spoke German and did not speak Yiddish at all. She cooked Susan foods she didn't like. Worst of all, Susan always felt like Caixai was... watching her, and not in the way a nanny should. On her 10th birthday in 1940, Susan had a small party attended mostly by her own parents. That night Nanny Caixia came to tuck her into bed, but she brought a strange blanket with her. It was made out of a weird black fabric that Susan couldn't place. With a strange little half smile, Caixia told Susan that this was a special blanket, a magical blanket, that would keep her safe at night. Too afraid to protest, Susan remained still as Caixia tucked her in tightly with the blanket, even covering her head. As soon as she was completely covered, Susan felt the blanket change... it was no longer a blanket, it was void. As she fell through the hole in reality she screamed for her parents, but no human heard her.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> Susan's new life was one of utter terror. The One Who Whispers from the Outside was a strange and terrible True Fae, incomprehensible to even others of his own kind. He took the form of a great bat-winged thing made of darkness and light from fading stars, many times larger than a grown man, let alone a 10-year-old girl. The One Who Whispers from the Outside made some changes to Susan; he covered her in the same darkness that was his own substance, and he stole away a little bit of her real name, giving her one that was just slightly off.<br /> <br /> The One Who Whispers from the Outside was very lonely, for whatever purpose he served in Arcadia was one that did not bring him much into contact with others. So he stole little girls and turned them into his wives. Suzan was one of these. He never spoke... except when he lay down with his wives at night. Oh, he never touched the girls, other than to sidle up against them in the perpetual darkness of his realm. He would whisper to them though, his bat-fanged mouth full of darkness hovering over their ears. He told them secrets, dark things that even he could not bear to know alone. Things about the true nature of the Wyrd, about what was really out there in the night sky.<br /> <br /> The One Who Whispers from the Outside was not devoid of compassion, however. He knew that the burdens he had to share with his dear little wives were taxing. He made each one a promise; they would listen to his secrets for seven years, and then if they wanted, he would let them go back to the real world. Suzan suffered in silence for those 7 years, but when the time came for her &quot;marriage&quot; to end, she jumped at the chance. The One Who Whispers from the Outside spent one more night with his beloved wife, tenderly whispering his darkest knowledge to her on their final night together, and in the morning she awoke in an unoccupied bedroom of a Hong Kong whorehouse.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Suzan was 17 and half mad when she returned to the human world. It was fortuitous, then, that the whorehouse she woke up in was owned and operated by the Dai Lo Dai of the Hong Kong Phantom Tong. Jishen Wu took Suzan under his wing, rebuilding her broken psyche over the course of the next year, into something usable, something slightly criminal. Suzan tried to look for her family during this year, and for any sign of a Fetch, but she couldn't find them. Jishen mentioned that shortly after she had been taken, the Japanese had occupied the city and it was possible her family had been killed. He'd never heard of anyone named Caixia.<br /> <br /> In 1948, Suzan was ready to serve as an instrument of the Tong. With the skills in criminal undertakings she had learned at the foot of the Tong's leaders, combined with the mystical knowledge she had learned during her Durance, Jishen knew she would be able to spread the destabilizing mission of the Tong beyond the borders of East Asia. Flush with cash to set up her own operations, Suzan was loaded onto a plane and sent to Port Newark with standing orders to set up the Tong on the eastern seaboard of the United States.<br /> <br /> For a year, Suzan did as she was told, still somewhat dazed by how quickly her life was advancing. With King Bogumil on his deathbed and her power in the underworld firmly established, however, something clicked in Suzan's mind. She didn't need to be anyone's lackey or concubine any more. Putting together her own personal talent at deception with her network of criminal informers, she dug up some deeply buried dirt on Tomasz Niezychowski, King Bogumil's likely successor (something having to do with being a secret informant for the Nazis in occupied Poland). She blackmailed him out of royal office and seized the throne for herself at the tender age of 20, all without the approval of her Phantom Tong &quot;superiors&quot; back in Hong Kong.<br /> <br /> From that day forward she has ruled over the Moon Court of the Freehold of the Palisades with an equal combination of deception, black magic, and criminal wealth. With the Great Schism of the Sun Court in the late 1990s, Secret Suzan was the first to realize that without a King of the Sun, the Freehold was in danger from the True Fae. She brokered the Truce and sees to enforcing it when it looks like one side (usually the Modernists) is going to push out the other faction. This has the added benefit of keeping the Freehold fairly destabilized. In truth, however, Suzan secretly prefers the Orthodox Sun to the Modernist. Suzan's criminal rackets all rely on people with traditional notions of morality being disgusted by her and her supporter's activities. If people think that taking hard drugs is a simple lifestyle choice, or prostitution is sanitized as &quot;sex work,&quot; almost the entire Moon Court in the Freehold will have to change its paradigm on Glamour collection. It would be destabilizing for her own personal rule, and that is one form of instability Suzan is not willing to see come to pass.<br /> <br /> Suzan's open rulership of the Freehold did not go over well with her Phantom Tong elders back in Hong Kong. In the mid 1980's her reduction of standing with them forced her to relinquish the title of Dai Lo Dai of the Freehold to another. These so-called Dai Lo Dais tend to not last very long if they openly antagonize her. Thankfully the current holder of the title, Salacious L, is her viceroy and a member of her inner circle.<br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> After her Durance and experiences with the Phantom Tong, whatever was left of the innocent little Susan Goldner is long gone. Secret Suzan is a hard woman, and she does not suffer fools gladly. There is a terrible wildness lurking just behind her eyes, and though she has never been known to indulge it, its very threat keeps many people in line. As she has advanced in years and in power, she has developed many enemies. It is a well known fact that, in order to protect herself, she has mostly withdrawn into her Hollow, and only interacts with her trusted seconds. What is not quite so well known is that her mastery of various contracts makes her almost impossible to recognize or find if she does not want to be found. Many Changelings, both Moon and Sun, would be surprised how much Suzan gets around while disguised.<br /> <br /> Suzan tries to maintain as careful balance between keeping the Freehold &quot;unstable&quot; as is required by her Phantom Tong ideology, and making sure that her own reign is secure. The key word there is &quot;tries.&quot; In reality, Suzan clearly favors her own power and position over loyalty to any ideology. She hasn't been drummed out of the Phantom Tong yet (in no little part because there are plenty of Dai Los just like her), but she is not popular with Tong &quot;purists.&quot;<br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231VnE2OGR6MU1vWGs/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>Suzan's Hollow requires its own explanation. The Hollow is called the <i>Villa Claustrophobia</i>. It was given to her by Jishen Wu when he inserted her into the Freehold; she does not know how he learned of it or where it came from (a secret that she is very frustrated she has never been able to learn). The interior of the Hollow appears as an early 20th century mansion, with some simple electricity and refrigeration, running hot and cold water, and even a couple of rotary phones that somehow connect into the real phone network.<br /> <br /> The Villa has no exterior- about two inches away from every window is a wall of hard, gray, cracked concrete. The only openings that do not lead to this wall of concrete are the Hollow's three gates. The first gate, which opens into the Villa's grand foyer, is a disused entrance to a boiler room located on the outside of a seedy strip club in Perth Amboy. The door is around the corner from the front of the strip joint in a trash-strewn alley whose only occupants are rats and the occasional drunk taking a piss. This gate cannot be accessed at all while the sun is up. While the sun is set, anyone who carries an amethyst (of any size, even a small shard will do) on his person can pass through to the Villa Claustrophobia. The second gate is located in the kitchen of the Villa, and from the inside of the Hollow takes the form of the midden door. This gate opens into a small cave next to a stream in Stokes Forest. It is one-way only and is primarily used by the Villa's staff to get rid of trash, though people can exit from this gate as well. The final gate is known only to Secret Suzan; it is a hidden escape tunnel that is located in her sleeping chambers. This gate leads to a storage closet in the old graduate school building of Princeton University. To enter the Villa through this gate, the user must recite Job 2: 9-10 (in any real language) before opening the door and stepping through.<br /> <br /> The Villa Claustrophobia has a number of minor magical conveniences (like electricity, water, and phone service, even though it should be impossible). The larders and refrigerators of the Hollow restock themselves with fresh food at sundown on Friday, every week. Despite being completely enclosed in concrete, the Villa never runs out of air (though there is a pervasive smell of stale air). The Villa is served by a troupe of 14 anthropomorphic mice (they're all about 3 feet tall) that clean, cook, and wait on Secret Suzan and her guests. In addition to this, th Villas has two major magical qualities.<br /> <br /> <i>The Throne of Velvet Power: </i>The Villa contains a throne room with a grand oaken throne cushioned with black crushed velvet. Anyone who posses a Mantle rating from the Autumn, Winter, Moon, or East courts that is sitting on the throne gains the Rote quality on all Manipulation rolls to an audience that shares his mantle. If the audience is people with different mantles, no mantles, or a mixed audience, the bonus on the Manipulation roll is simply a 9-again roll. The Throne cannot be removed from its position in the throne room by any known power, but it is otherwise a normal chair, and can be destroyed or harmed. Anything that does serious harm to the chair destroys its magic forever.<br /> <br /> <i>The Mirror of Objectification: </i>This ornate mirror hangs in its own special room. The mirror can produce a scrying sensor in the real world anywhere within the New York metropolitan area. To place a sensor, the user must speak a location (and only a location, telling the mirror to scry on a person always fails) and make a Manipulation + Occult roll. The scrying sensor will last for 5 minutes per success on the roll. After the scrying has finished, the mirror must recharge for 1 hour per success rolled on the Manipulation+Occult roll. The scrying sensor can be moved by the Mirror's user; it can move anywhere in the designated location, and up to 15 yards away from the boundary or exterior of the location. The user simply tells the mirror where to move the sensor, ie. &quot;Turn left 90 degrees and move six feet&quot;. The sensor has the same visual acumen as a human with 20/20 vision, moves at an average human walking pace, and can be told to zoom in in order to read text or get closeups.<br /> <br /> The Mirror can only see objects and plants, not any kind of animal, human, or supernatural being. Anything worn or closely held by such beings vanishes as well. The mirror emits audio as well, but it cannot hear anything it does not have the ability to see. It can, however, hear recordings of such beings.<br /> <br /> There is one exception to these rules: the Mirror can see and hear Prometheans and other creatures that use Pyros or Azoth just fine. Prometheans appear in their human disguise, but if a exceptional success is rolled on the Mirror's Manipulation+Occult roll, their Disfigurement is revealed as well. Secret Suzan, in using the mirror, has glimpse a Promethean one or two times (though she never saw their Disfigurements). Though her skill in Occult is high enough to be vaguely aware that Prometheans exist, she has no idea that that's what her magic mirror saw.<br /> <br /> Much like the Throne of Velvet Power, the Mirror cannot be removed by any known force from the wall on which it hangs. It is otherwise, however, exactly like a normal mirror, and if shattered or otherwise seriously harmed, its magic is gone forever.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Suzan's mother tongue is Yiddish; she can speak German, English and Cantonese fluently, but somewhat to her embarrassment she has trouble reading these languages and cannot write them at all.<br /> <br /> Suzan's frailty is that she can't ever compliment, praise, or affirm someone to his face.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-04-2015, 03:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-04T19:24:10-08:00","id":359823,"title":"","content":"<i>The Viceroy of Eclipses</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Kevin Lai<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Fu Look, officially, but goes by his nickname Salacious L to most people.<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Salacious L, some Changelings also know his &quot;real&quot; name Fu Look.<br /> <br /> Titles: Viceroy of Eclipses, Commandant of Fort Lee, Dai Lo Dai of the Phantom Tong<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Pebbles - Mercedes Boy <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XXZAm2NlMEE\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XXZAm2NlMEE</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> Salacious L is a Chinese man in his late 20's or early 30's, but more importantly, he is probably one of the most physically attractive men you have ever seen. Everything is perfectly symmetrical. He's in excellent shape but isn't outrageously muscular. His lustrous black hair is never out of place, there's a mischievous twinkle in his eye, and an arrogant-but-irresistible smirk on his lips. Salacious L is a pimp, a very successful one, and dresses in expensive, slightly outlandish suits and gold jewelery that reflect this. Underneath all of this he has a series of very well done tattoos that <i>would </i>mark him as a member of a Hong Kong-based triad, but the symbols refer to no group that any actual gang member would know.<br /> <br /> <b><b>Mien: </b></b><span style=\"font-size:13px\">In his true form, Salacious L's skin becomes a dark, navy-blue color. His tattoos vanish and are replaced with what look like tiny diamonds embedded in his skin, that catch the light and sparkle like tiny stars. If the moonlight catches his eyes in just the right way, they appear clouded over or even translucent, but just for a moment.</span><br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> Kevin Lai was born in Manhattan's Chinatown, and grew up just across the river in Englewood Cliffs, New Jersey. His parents were from Hong Kong, originally, but had immigrated to the United States seeking better economic opportunities in the mid 1970s. His father was an electrical engineer and his mother a home-maker; both were active in their local Chinese Methodist church.<br /> <br /> Kevin Lai was not much to look at as a young man- slightly pudgy, wan skin. His parents kept him away from high-status activities in favor of more studies, music lessons (that he was terrible at), and church groups in the hopes that he would eventually become a doctor, lawyer, or engineer. He came to resent the beautiful people who were so obviously inferior to himself, but who always got what he wanted. There was little he could do about it though. When he was 15, in 1997, his parents sent him to a Methodist sleep away camp in upstate New York for the summer. It was there that he met Amy Chan, and it was there that she would reject his clumsy teenage advances. Embarrassed beyond all knowing, he ran out into the woods behind the dormitories.<br /> <br /> Salacious L doesn't exactly remember what happened, because he was bawling and his eyes were filled with tears, but he knows he was hit over the head with something heavy and blunt.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> The Recombiner was not like the Kindly Ones that other people talk about. It wasn't a person... it was more like a place. A collection of cartoonish Rube Goldberg machines that none-the-less did terrible things. It would continually process and re-process Changelings, taking them apart and putting them back together in the most painful way possible. Kevin Lai was subject to this mad treatment for five years. Each day he would go into the machine, and after being poked, prodded, pulled, sliced, and pinched, he'd pop out the other end completely different. One day he was Gristlegrinder. The next a Manikin. The next a Leechfinger/Bright One. The Recombiner didn't just change his Seeming or Kith though. Sometimes he'd come out as a tall blond-haired, blue eyed German, others as a drooling hunchback pygmy. Once or twice he came out as a woman.<br /> <br /> Then, one day, he emerged from the output end of the Recombiner and looked in the mirrors the surrounded the holding pens where the Recombiner's &quot;inputs&quot; were stored between sessions. And he saw that he was beautiful... exactly as he imagined himself being in his dreams. At that moment, one of the mirrors silently slid aside, revealing a long straight tunnel that went so far he couldn't see the end. Kevin didn't need any more encouragement and took off running. It wasn't long before he found himself back in his home town, Englewood Cliffs, emerging from a toilet stall in the public park.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Kevin's first order of business was to kill his Fetch. He didn't want to be reminded of what he had been, or what he suspected he would become. He was right- the fetch was a boring, fat, ugly, sexually frustrated Computer Sciences graduate student. The Recombiner had left Kevin colder inside, and he had no problem planning or carrying out his Fetch's murder. He even watched the funeral from afar, to see his parents one last time as they grieved over their poor boy, shot during a drive-by intended for gangsters but gone horribly awry. They wouldn't even recognize Kevin now, he was so different, so he decided that would be the last he saw of them.<br /> <br /> It was the first he would see of Secret Suzan, however. She approached Kevin after his Fetch's death, impressed at how easily he had orchestrated it. She said that she was having a bit of trouble with one of her rivals- the Dai Lo Dai of the Phantom Tong. It seemed that the Changeling was put there by the Entitlement's elders in Hong Kong, and he and Suzan didn't see eye to eye. If Kevin could eliminate the rival for her, the Queen of the Moon would reward him handsomely.<br /> <br /> It took a while, but Lai managed to arrange for the Dai Lo Dai's death, completely free of any connection to Secret Suzan, in an unfortunate boating accident. Secret Suzan is many things, but she doesn't renege on deals. Kevin got a new identity, a high position in both the Phantom Tong and the Freehold, and control of the prostitution ring that his &quot;predecessor&quot; had previously under his control. Originally just going by the street name &quot;L,&quot; he eventually became known as a &quot;Salacious L&quot; for the ease with which he could pick up women.<br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> Salacious L revels in his position as Secret Suzan's envoy, and when needs must, enforcer. He's also technically the highest ranking Phantom Tong member in the Freehold, outranking even Secret Suzan. To Changeling &quot;newbies&quot; Salacious L plays up the &quot;vain, incompetent playboy&quot; act, but anyone who has been around the Freehold knows that he is, in fact, a vain <i>extremely competent </i>playboy, and not to be trifled with.<br /> <br /> When he's not working out, lounging around one of his &quot;massage parlors&quot; (his prostitution ring is based out of Fort Lee), he is hitting up the best hotspots he can to pick up women. Salacious L isn't lusty, really- its not the sex he's after. He's much more excited about people appreciating his beauty and wealth. He has a habit of occasionally going out in public (even outside of Halloween) with the Mien on full display so that he can wow people. He figures that he's literally a pimp, so he might as well make the most of that reputation for eccentricity.<br /> <br /> At the moment, Salacious L is fully loyal to Secret Suzan. Still, if her organization looked like it was going down, he'd serious contemplate jumping ship and making his own play for King of the Moon.<br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231NlRRSjBsbVo3cG8/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>Character sheet is mostly self-explanatory. Salacious L's skill in occult is the result of his direct tutelage by Secret Suzan.<br /> <br /> Tomorrow will be write-ups on the Freehold's Goblin Markets.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-04-2015, 10:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T06:38:06-08:00","id":360168,"title":"","content":"I find it interesting/disturbing that the two highest ranked members of the Moon Court were released rather than escaped.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T17:49:57-08:00","id":360792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ZealousChristian24</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play?p=360168#post360168\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find it interesting/disturbing that the two highest ranked members of the Moon Court were released rather than escaped.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I hope I made it clear that neither of them are loyalists/privateers, but yes, their old Keepers might still be watching [dun dun dun...]","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-05T19:56:25-08:00","id":360870,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Goblin Markets in the Freehold of the Palisades</b></span><br /> <br /> The Freehold of the Palisades is home to two major Goblin Markets. The first, Pinochle's Emporium, is officially unofficially sponsored by the Court of the Sun. The second, the Pulaski Skyway Market, is unofficially officially sponsored by the Court of the Moon.<br /> <br /> <i>Pinochle's Emporium</i><br /> <br /> Pinochle's Emporium opens one or two days a month, always from noon to sunset, and always on a day that is a multiple of the number 7. To find out if the Emporium is going to be held, Changelings in the Freehold must find a copy of the <i>Daily Princetonian; </i>if the Emporium is going to be held on the next day, the paper will contain a reference to the game pinochle somewhere in its pages. Alternatively, Changelings can just show up at the Springdale Golf Course clubhouse, which is the location of the market, on days of the month that are multiples of 7 and hope the market is open. The clubhouse is always mysteriously closed to the &quot;public&quot; (aka regular humans) on the days that the Emporium is held, and Changelings (and sometimes other creatures aware of the supernatural world) are allowed to browse its wares.<br /> <br /> The market is run by a very strange hob named Pinochle. Pinochle appears as a very short (about 4 1/2 feet tall) plump man with the long neck and head of a duck, all dressed in colorful robes. Perhaps more oddly, Pinochle seems to be generally friendly and helpful. In fact, many Changelings find him a bit <i>too </i>chummy and obsequious. Pinochle is a chatterbox and is possessed of a voice not dissimilar to that of Gilbert Gottfried. Otherwise, the vendors in the Emporium change from time to time. Pinochle is happy to have Changelings from the Freehold open booths at his Emporium as long as they abide by the rules he's hammered out with the Court of the Sun (see below), and have asked his permission to sell at a previous Emporium.<br /> <br /> Pinochle does not always have his own stall, claiming that he's just &quot;supervising&quot;, but he does occasionally open one. He always sells high quality dream vessels in the form of children's toys that invariably contain extremely pleasant dreams featuring popular children's cartoon characters (both from film and television). Pinochle will take payment in Glamour that his kind usually takes, but he'll also trade the vessels for copies of new (within the past 2 years) animated children's television shows or movies. Unfortunately, the shows <i>must </i>be recorded on a betamax tape, usually making it easier to pay in Glamour. For technically minded Changelings who want the challenge of putting <i>Frozen </i>onto a betamax tape, the opportunity is there.<br /> <br /> Metropolitan Maurikios takes a dim view of Goblin Markets in general, so during his reign he generally forbids Changelings from selling at the Emporium (though he knows it would be folly to try to stop them from buying). Consequently, Pinochle is a political supporter of Chris Grand, and will happily and loudly proclaim this preference for anyone to hear if the topic comes up.<br /> <br /> Other than this change in rules that takes place between the reigns of Chris Grand and Maurikios, the following agreement has been put in place between Pinochle and the Sun Court:<br /> <br /> - Absolutely no vice merchants<br /> <br /> - No tokens that have very dangerous catches (generally meaning only 1 and 2 dot tokens are available for sale)<br /> <br /> - No slave merchants or sale of sapient beings of any kind<br /> <br /> - No goblin fruits that have solely harmful effects (for example, a fruit that has no purpose other than to be used as a poison)<br /> <br /> - Dream vessels cannot contain nightmares<br /> <br /> - No sale of supernatural merchandise &quot;proper&quot; to other supernatural ecologies (ie. no Supernal artifacts, no Sekhem relics, etc)- Changelings just don't need that kind of heat<br /> <br /> Pinochle scrupulously abides by this agreement and vets each merchant to make sure that he is not selling something forbidden by the compact. Pinochle is, however, really as kind and helpful as his demeanor suggests, and cannot fathom that any of his vendors might be less than sterlingly moral individuals. Consequently, his Emporium has occasionally played host to loyalist, privateer, and even disguised True Fae vendors, all there to scope out the Freehold and perhaps mark targets for later.<br /> <br /> <i>Pulaski Skyway Market</i><br /> <br /> In the darkest part of the night, from 3 AM till first light, the Goblin Market known only by its location opens. The Pulaski Skyway market is held only on Friday the 13th, limiting it to occurring at most three times a year. Unlike Pinochle's Emporium, there are no rules at the Pulaski Skyway Market and everything and anything is up for sale, making this market the &quot;unofficial&quot; market of the Moon Court.<br /> <br /> This market takes place mostly inside of the Hedge; the &quot;gate&quot; is the entire length of the Pulaski Skyway. To enter, a prospective shopper must wear black mittens (not gloves- mittens) and a black mask that leaves only his eyes and mouth open- a ski mask will do. Then, he must pass underneath the Skyway from north to south. The hobs that run the market set up their stalls all along the 3 mile length of the Skyway on the Hedge-side (though some do open up in the more concealed areas of the human-world side). These hobs, and their guards, all have a decidedly sinister aspect- they all appear as creatures made of pure shadow with glowing white eyes and teeth like tiny crescent moons. For reasons the hobs refuse to give, their shadowy guards will throw out any shopper who doesn't keep his mittens or mask on all the time, even in the heat of summer. The exceptions to this are Changelings who have a Mantle rating 3 or higher from the Moon, Winter, Autumn, or East courts. They are permitted to remove their masks (but the mittens must stay on).<br /> <br /> As mentioned above, all sorts of things can be found at the Pulaski Skyway Market, from something as innocent as an old pulp fiction novel, to as monstrous as a child slave. The market is known especially for its Gluttony vice merchants, who have street food stalls that produce food so delectable and in such quantities that even many people who don't have Gluttony as a vice can't help but overindulge.<br /> <br /> The shadow hobs that run the market don't allow any Changelings or any other sort of being to get in on their business- with one exception.<br /> <br /> As long as anyone can remember, the Market has been host to a vendor who gives his name as Mr. Debonair. Mr. Debonair is anything but what his name suggests- he is a very boring and average looking white man in a plain black suit with a white shirt and a black tie- very &quot;men in black&quot;- who talks in a bored, monotone voice. Older Changelings will recall that in the 70's and 80's, Mr. Debonair was a boring, average black man. Even older Changelings will say that before <i>that </i>he was a boring, average white man but not the same boring, average white man that he is now. Mr. Debonair won't talk about himself except in the vaguest terms and quickly steers any conversation back to business. He sells typical Changeling fare at his stall- Tokens, dream vessels, hedgespun clothes, the usual. However, his stall is unusual for two reasons: one is that he'll only trade like for like- Tokens for Tokens, clothes for clothes, etc. Secondly, he'll occasionally have something from far left field- a seemingly normal item that isn't a token, but that Changelings (especially those attuned to the Wyrd) can tell is not right. A picture of a building that everyone knows, but somehow covered in weird wiring or technology. A translation key for a numbers station that can only be picked up when there have been 4 or more murders in Brooklyn the day before.<br /> <br /> Mr. Debonair is an Analyst Demon. He was inserted into the world by the God Machine when the Pulaski Skyway was first built with the mission of simply observing it and making twice monthly reports by dropping his encoded summary in a trashcan in Jersey City just outside of city hall. For reasons that are (probably) completely unrelated to everyone's favorite insane demiurge/divine computer program, the Shadow Hobs decided to set up their market under the Pulaski Skyway at the same time Mr. Debonair was given the mission of watching over it.<br /> <br /> Mr. Debonair was hooked. He found the Changelings and their commercial activities underneath the bridge endlessly fascinating. He was sure the God Machine would too, but he never heard anything back from his creator, and he never received any changes in his mission parameters. In 1942, he fell when he decided he couldn't just watch anymore. He needed to participate in the glorious commerce. The Hobs seemed not to notice him when he set up his little stall with his &quot;seed money&quot; of a couple of Changeling artifacts he managed to &quot;acquire&quot; (ie, pry from the extremely dead hands of their previous owners).<br /> <br /> Mr. Debonair does not have any connection with an Agency or a ring. He bears no ill will toward the God Machine, and in fact still makes his twice monthly report on the Pulaski Skyway and everything that happens near it. He's never been troubled by an Angel and he doesn't expect to ever be. Consequently, he has a small amount of status with the Integrator agenda. However, his focus on the Changeling world coupled with his Integrator ideology means that his covers tend to be paper thin and can easily be blown wide open if someone wants to cause him trouble. Thankfully that hasn't happened yet, and because he's a creature of inertia, he expects that it never will.<br /> <br /> Debonair's desire to experiment with something he was just suppose to observe caused him to fall, and he sometimes gets the itch to do so again. It's on these days he'll offer a God Machine related item for sale- a gadget, or something that reveals a bit of infrastructure, just to see what Changelings would be willing to trade him for these oddities (that they often can't even use). He's been doing this for over 70 years and it hasn't blown up in his face yet, but when it does, there will likely be Hell to pay, both by Mr. Debonair and by the Changeling who has purchased his demonic goods.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Tomorrow night, I'll likely have up nearby Freeholds and perhaps an important Courtless character.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-05-2015, 11:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-06T17:17:15-08:00","id":361762,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>Nearby Freeholds</b></span></span><br /> <br /> The Freehold of the Palisades lies near the heart of the New York Metropolitan area. This is a brief description of the Freeholds nearby and a rumor or plot-hook about them should my players decide to visit.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of the Pine Barrens</i><br /> <br /> This Freehold generally encompasses what would be considered &quot;South Jersey.&quot; The Changelings in this area are reclusive in the extreme, and those few who visit the Freehold of the Palisades tend to evince relatively low Clarity scores. The major reason for this is that the Pine Barrens, and indeed most forests of any size in South Jersey, have a much higher than average occurrence of Hollows. The Winter, Autumn, and Summer courts of the Pine Barrens all have their &quot;royal courts&quot; inside of their own large Hollows, and many of their courtiers literally live in the Hedge year round.<br /> <br /> The exception to this is their Spring Court, which maintains its physical presence in Cape May, and whose courtiers tend to be slightly saner.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of the City</i><br /> <br /> This is the Freehold that covers Manhattan. The Spring Queen and the Winter King were once married, but have recently had a bitter divorce over reasons that aren't well understood in the Palisades. The divorce has left a great deal of political tension in the Freehold, not in the least because the Spring Queen and the Winter King had, against all odds, a small human(-like, for now) child together. He's 4 1/2.<br /> <br /> The Freehold of the City has many Changelings who work in glamorous (in the mundane sense) and high-powered jobs, and consequently a high proportion of them are members of the Satrapy of Pearls or the Guild of Goldspinners (about 10% of the Freehold are members of these orders).<br /> <br /> <i>Chinatown</i><br /> <br /> Manhattan's Chinatown is a very special case; it is not its own Freehold, but it's also not part of the Freehold of the City. Chinatown can best be described as a colony of Hong Kong's East Court, whose members make up the majority of the neighborhood's fae population. The Freehold of the City's seasonal monarchs are very unhappy with this situation, but every time they've tried to dislodge the Hong Kong East courtiers, the foreign court has poured so much mundane money into defending the area that they just couldn't compete (even with all of the money they control- which is a substantial amount).<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of the Bronx</i><br /> <br /> The Bronx is host to a typical Seasonal Court system, but the Winter Court is notably more powerful than the others because of their connections to human gangs. Winter's power was also greatly amplified because, in the wake of the 9/11/2001 terrorist attacks, the monarchs of the other three seasonal courts were unmasked as Banished Kindly Ones by the current Winter King.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of the Queens</i><br /> <br /> By unspoken compact, all of the seasonal monarchs of Queens must literally be Queens- they have never had a male monarch and probably never will. The Queens Autumn Court throws a Halloween ball that is <i>the </i>Changeling social event of the season, and any Changeling who is anyone in the New York area must attend. Even Secret Suzan, or at least someone shape-changed to look like Secret Suzan, is known to show up.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of the Kings</i><br /> <br /> Much like its sister borough, Brooklyn is only ruled by monarchs of a single sex, but in this case, the sex is male. The Brooklyn courts are mostly ceremonial- about 75% of the Changelings in Brooklyn are intentionally courtless and the governing system is very weak. Each of the seasonal monarchs is elected from amongst the male populace of the Freehold on a rolling four year basis with universal Changeling suffrage (meaning that there is an election every year). Any male can stand for election, and if he's not part of the court, he will swear fealty to it while he's in office.<br /> <br /> Entitlements are rare in Brooklyn and Freehold-specific noble titles outside of the Monarchs themselves are almost unheard of.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of Coney Island</i><br /> <br /> This tiny little former island (now a peninsula thanks to land fill) consists of the neighborhoods of Sea Gate, Coney Island, Brighton Beach, and Manhattan Beach. It broke away from Freehold of the Kings in the late 1970's, and a court was established around the Crepuscular Courts. The overall political weakness of the Brooklyn freehold saw to it that they were able to do so. Dawn Court holds sway from Midnight to Noon, and Dusk from Noon to Midnight, every day. The small number of Changelings who reside in the Freehold of Coney Island are considered eccentric even by Changeling standards, and they rarely leave their little island demesne.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of Richmond</i><br /> <br /> Richmond was the former name of Staten Island, and its Changeling Freehold retains that name. There are seasonal courts in Richmond, by the island is lousy with Magistrates of the Wax Mask, who are all required to remain courtless in order to &quot;maintain neutrality.&quot; In reality, the island's <i>Magister Equitum </i>is the real political power on the island, and he wants all of his subordinates in the Entitlement loyal to him and the Entitlement itself, not to a seasonal court.<br /> <br /> That's not to say the Magisters are corrupt or incompetent, they're (usually) not, but it can be a bit of a shock to foreign Changelings to see how weak the seasonal monarchs are before they learn the truth of whose word carries weight in the Freehold.<br /> <br /> <i>Freehold of Long Island</i><br /> <br /> The Freehold of Long Island covers Nassau and Suffolk counties. In this Freehold, the Winter, Spring, and Summer monarchs may reign when their season comes up, but they do not rule. True political power lies with the Autumn Queen year round. Though an experienced and powerful occultist herself, everyone knows her power over the others is due to her alliance with two human witches.<br /> <br /> What most people do not know is just how powerful those witches are- they are Seer of the Throne Tetrarchs, the male twin in service to the Ruin, the female twin in service to the Prophet. The direction of the Freehold has something to do with an Omen they are constructing for their eventual Supernal ascension, but only the wisest of master mages would be able to tell a Changeling about Archmastery at all, let alone guess at what the twins are attempting.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-13-2015, 12:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-11T12:57:02-08:00","id":366557,"title":"","content":"Hey, I just want to say that I'm loving this thread of yours. I've just made an account to more closely follow the Changeling stuff: I'm currently away from my long-term groups and am feeling the RP withdrawal. Things like this help. So thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4722-birnam","name":"Birnam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4722_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-12T19:29:58-08:00","id":368173,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Birnam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/356914-freehold-of-the-palisades-setting-actual-play?p=366557#post366557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey, I just want to say that I'm loving this thread of yours. I've just made an account to more closely follow the Changeling stuff: I'm currently away from my long-term groups and am feeling the RP withdrawal. Things like this help. So thanks!</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks, I know the feeling. I'm trying to put together a group from my friends at my LGS, who are all wargamers or TCG players. Not exactly the easiest feat but I think I can rope a few of them into it.<br /> <br /> I will have some more stuff up on two important courtless characters soon that I intend to have be early allies of the PCs. I have the ideas in my head but at the moment haven't written much down. I might get something up tonight.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-12T23:20:53-08:00","id":368339,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>The Courtless in the Freehold of the Palisades</b></span><br /> <br /> About 25% of the Changelings in the Freehold don't belong to any court. Their reasons are as varied as the stars in the sky; some of them don't agree with a court system at all, some just don't like the Sun/Moon courts in particular (but don't immigrate away for personal reasons), others think that not joining a court will save them from politics intruding onto their already tumultuous lives.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking, people who want to remain Courtless are permitted to do so so long as they take a slightly modified &quot;Good Neighbors&quot; pledge to one of the Freehold's Monarchs or a suitable stand-in, who represents the whole Freehold. At that time they are considered subjects of the Freehold but are not beholden to (or derive any benefit from) court membership. Those who refuse to take such a pledge must tread carefully because they are considered outlaws. The Moon Court, especially Phantom Tong members, are wont to go soft on these outlaws. Conversely, Modernist Sun courtiers tend to be the harshest on both oath-bound and outlaw Courtless (if you don't stand on the side of Progress, you stand against it, after all). Orthodox Sun courtiers are tolerant of those that take the Good Neighbors pledge but usually unforgiving of those that don't. Again, personal reactions may be very different than the &quot;norm&quot; described here.<br /> <br /> Those who do join up with a court take a variant of the Commendation pledge that replaces the Good Neighbors pledge, while those given a noble title are expected to take the Knight's Oath.<br /> <br /> Both Sun and Moon look somewhat askance at Entitlement members who refuse to join a court, with the exception of Blackbird Bishops and Magistrates of the Wax Mask. Secret Suzan, in particular, does not tolerate Phantom Tong members who will not join the Moon Court in a timely manner.<br /> <br /> Here are two Courtless NPCs that I intend to shepherd the PCs into the Changeling way of life, or at least work as early allies.<br /> <br /> <i>The Little Bird of Manville</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Karen Blechacz<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Karen Blechacz<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Karen &quot;Little Bird&quot; Blechacz<br /> <br /> Titles: Blackbird Bishop<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Paula Cole - I Don't Want to Wait <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dDWp-zek4Ic\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dDWp-zek4Ic</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> Karen is a sturdy woman of Eastern European descent in her late 20's, with dark blond hair and a heart shaped face. She's otherwise unremarkable, and tends to dress in work jeans and flannel shirts or other serviceable clothes. She keeps her hair up and out of her face.<br /> <br /> <b><b>Mien: </b></b>In her true form, Karen's flesh becomes the light brown color and consistency of balsam wood, and her hair becomes the tiny needles of the balsam tree mixed intermittently with black feathers. In both her mien and her mask, the scent of fresh-cut balsam wood follows her everywhere. The scent is much stronger to those that can see her mien.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> Things were not going well for 16 year old Manville resident Karen Blechacz. Her father was long gone. Her mother lived at home, collecting a meager disability check for nebulous medical reasons rather than trying to find work. And, she was pregnant. The only thing going for her, almost miraculously, was that her boyfriend Carl Bauer decided that he was going to stay with her, and even said that he'd marry her after high school.<br /> <br /> The due date came, and the Bauer twins were born- one boy, and one girl. It was a moment of happiness in an otherwise hardscrabble life. It was not to last, however, because Carl and Karen drew the attention of a Keeper who loved to steal young couples away and turn them into shrubberies in his castle. He did so the very night the twins were born, leaving fetches in place.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> Karen doesn't remember the name or title of her Keeper, and she's pretty sure he never told her. He transformed Karen into a balsam tree in his garden, and set Carl to tend to her (which eventually led to Carl becoming a Wizened Woodwalker). All of the trees in the Keeper's garden were the same- two stolen lovers, the woman became the tree, the man became the tender. Occasionally, the Keeper would take a break from whatever he did in his castle and take a stroll in his garden of kidnapped women. He never spoke to the trees, and as far as Karen knows, he never spoke to the gardeners either. Carl cared tenderly for his tree-girlfriend, but sadly communication between the two was in short supply, though Karen was aware of everything that happened around her.<br /> <br /> Then, something Karen can't quite explain happened. It may have been her Keeper's doing in some kind of fit of pique, or, possibly, her Keeper had come under attack. A great windstorm whipped through his domain, shattering stone with lightning strikes and bowling over trees with hurricane force winds. Carl and the other gardeners had taken cover somewhere inside the castle. Karen was knocked over by the storm, and she feared that she would die. Instead, by cracking open her trunk and blowing away her branches, the storm revealed her arms, legs, and face. Running with the wind, she ran straight into the hedge and never looked back.<br /> <br /> That is, until she was stuck inside it's thorny embrace. She remember her love for Carl, how he had never left her side all those years, and tried to push her way back to Arcadia to bring him home with her. She failed, and became hopelessly lost. It was only the timely intervention of a Blackbird Bishop that saved her, bringing her out of the Hedge and back safely into Manville not far from where she had grown up.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Karen found that she had spent ten years as a tree in her Keeper's garden. Her fetch, and that of Carl's, had been terrible parents and were almost non functional as people. Her mother had kicked her out of their small home because they began abusing drugs and the children both. Fetch-Carl and Fetch-Karen lived a marginal existence in a trailer park, huffing meth when they could get it and watching daytime TV when they couldn't. They had never formally married, and the twins lived with Karen's mom. Again, the Blackbird Bishops came to Karen's rescue. They wouldn't kill the fetches for her, but they did give her the resources she needed to get on her feet and find someone who would - including a job as a shift manager at a Home Depot garden center.<br /> <br /> It wasn't long before both fetches were dead, and her sudden sobriety and job got her her kids back and repaired her relationship with her mother. She claimed that Carl had finally left her and gone way out of state to pursue the druggy life that she had given up. It wasn't that hard to believe, so everyone did, even Carl's parents. Karen owed everything to the Blackbird Bishops, so in order to repay her debts, she joined up. Most importantly, however, it gave her a chance to look for her beloved Carl, the real Carl, if he should ever find his way out into the Hedge.<br /> <br /> Karen, now known somewhat jokingly as &quot;Little Bird&quot; (she's what some might call a 'thick' woman), works as the chief agent of the Blackbird Bishops in Somerset County, where she scours the Hedge to help escaping Changelings get out, while also trying to get them on their feet once they're back in the real world.<br /> <br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> Little Bird is dedicated to the Blackbird Bishop's vocation, but she is a somewhat harried woman. She has to balance patrolling Somerset County's hedge, taking care of her now 12-year-old children, and maintaining her job at the Home Depot. Needless to say, she doesn't get too much sleep and can be a bit irritable. Deep down she's a kind person though, and her love for her almost-husband has never faded. Everything she does in the Blackbird Bishops is out of a deep hope that she'll be able to see him again.<br /> <br /> Little Bird has 0 time available as it is, so that means she has no time for Court politics. If anyone pressures her on the subject, she tells them to kindly fuck off. Her status as a Blackbird Bishop has prevented any reprisals so far, and she is actually pretty well liked by both Courts. Still, she is amenable to directing newly escaped Changelings to the appropriate Court if she feels it is what they would want.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231WHZxZWZkR1pjN1U/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>Character sheet is mostly self-explanatory. Karen's greatest weakness, perhaps, is that she had ensorcelled both her children, who share the Ancient Pact with her. This was a moment of weakness for her, because she simply could not balance all she has to do AND hiding her real self from her kids. They're taking it well so far, but it has the potential to open many problems in the future.<br /> <br /> Second character will be up tomorrow.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 01-22-2015, 11:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-13T05:49:46-08:00","id":368543,"title":"","content":"I like her! Seems like a good ally to have on one's side.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-15T23:26:50-08:00","id":371995,"title":"","content":"<i>The Arcadian Chef</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Jason DiMena<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Chef, Chef Montserrat<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Montserrat<br /> <br /> Titles: None<br /> <br /> Theme Song: The Beatles - Savoy Truffle <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=erg1aRORJX0\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=erg1aRORJX0</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> Now in his late 60's, Montserrat appears as an average Italian-American man with olive colored-skin tending towards the fairer end of that spectrum, graying hair, and some wrinkles about his eyes and ears. He is in average shape for a man of his age and otherwise does not possess strong characteristics except for his pronounced Roman nose. Monstserrat can usually be found in his chef's whites, but otherwise does not dress unusually for an older American man of average means.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> Montserrat's true face takes his human mask and adds what seems to be another ten years of wrinkles and graying hair. His face looks like it has been blackened by standing over a cooking fire and is constantly stained by soot that can never be washed away. His hands are covered in white flour that seems to be exuded by his skin; to those that can see Monserrat's mien, this flour gets everywhere, but fades from reality a minute or two later.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> In 1968, a fast paced job in one of Manhattan's top restaurants (two Michelin stars and working on the third) was all Jason DiMena, an Italian boy who grew up in Camden, could ever want. The hours were long and the rest of the kitchen staff could be pretty mean, but it was rare for someone of his age to be given a place on the line in such a fine establishment.<br /> <br /> Needless to say, Jason was very excited when the head pastry chef couldn't make it in because of the flu, and he had to fill in for his boss. It would finally be his chance to shine to the head chef, and he did. Apparently the restaurant was hosting some kind of VIP that nobody was quite able to describe. After the dessert courses were over, the maitre'd came in beaming and said the special guest was very pleased with the pastry assortment, and that he even extended his compliments to the cook- Jason<br /> <br /> Jason left the kitchen that night beaming, sure that a promotion was in order, but he didn't even make it to the nearest subway station. Something with huge claws, pointy teeth, and... formal dinner attire leapt out of the proverbial alley and dragged its new chef into the Hedge with nothing but a muffled cry.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> Jason's new keeper was a horrendous ogre, all gristle and pointy teeth, that had pretensions of grandeur. It renamed all of its servants after &quot;fancy&quot; things, though it had no understanding of what was considered fancy or high-class by humans. Jason was re-christened Montserrat and forced to labor in the ogre's kitchens for an age as a pastry chef.<br /> <br /> The ogre invited other Gentry to its table, and while they all despised its inept and falsely-cultured grumblings, it DID kidnap the finest human chefs to prepare its meals. So, the gentry attended the ogre's feasts, and Montserrat was able to hob-nob and come as close as possible to having polite conversation with Gentry while he served them his delicious desserts. So successful were his dishes with the ogre's guests, that the ogre entrusted Montserrat with a magical golden spoon, able to create the finest meals.<br /> <br /> With the spoon in his possession and the ogre's trust, Montserrat was able to hatch a plan of escape. He poisoned all of the Gentry, including his ogre keeper, during one of the creature's feasts. Using the magic of the spoon, he folded the essence of sleep into his souffl\u00e9, and when the assembled guests ate it, they immediately fell into a deep, deep slumber. Montserrat absconded with the magical spoon and ran into his Keeper's herb garden, where he knew a door into the hedge was hidden. In short order he had found his way through the liminal dimension and emerged right behind the old pizza shop in Camden where he had his first job.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Montserrat took a &quot;job&quot; as the official chef of the Spring King of the Freehold of the Pine Barrens in Cape May. He used his magical spoon to great effect, preparing feasts for his new lord just as he did for his Keeper, only without the imminent threat of himself being on the menu should he fail. As it happened, Montserrat's polite chats with the Gentry during his durance helped given him a pretty good head for occult matters, and he ended up doubling as the King's go-to guy on mystical matters, something that the Spring Court was not very well known for.<br /> <br /> Things are never that simple, however. While Montserrat's keeper was not particularly cultured (except for his cultured palette), he was not stupid either. The magical spoon had a terrible drawback; the location of anyone who ate food cooked with it would be known to the great ogre for a year and a day afterwards. Many Changelings in the Pine Barrens ate of Montserrat's cooking, all prepared with his special spoon, and incidents of Changelings being re-kidnapped back into Arcadia rose in the Freehold precipitously. By the early 1980's, the freehold was in a panic, but a hero of the Autumn Court finally fingered Montserrat's magical spoon.<br /> <br /> Though the Spring King knew that Montserrat was free of any malice, he had no choice to levy a punishment on his head chef. Montserrat was banished from the Freehold and never permitted to return while giving up the spoon that gave him his magical powers.<br /> <br /> By this point Montserrat was too invested in the spoon to give it up, even if it did summon his old keeper. Meekly pretending to accept the punishment, he used his contracts of Forge to reshape an ordinary spoon to resemble his magical kitchen implement. He was long gone before the deception was discovered.<br /> <br /> Montserrat traveled around for many years, but, somewhat dangerously, eventually settled back in New Jersey, only in the Freehold of the Palisades. While he was never forced to relinquish membership in the Spring Court, and he never did, he is effectively courtless in the Palisades. Always careful to hide his presence as much as possible in order to avoid retribution from the Freehold of the Pine Barrens, Montserrat now &quot;owns&quot; the Melting Pot restaurant in Somerville through an ensorcelled retainer. He's also found a bit of political protection in bartering his services as a source of occult knowledge to Chris Grand, the leader of the Modernist Sun faction. which tends to lack people interested in these matters.<br /> <br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> Montserrat (he never goes by his real name, Jason DiMena) never really got over his Durance. He's completely fixated on his cooking, and it is the center of his whole life. Though he is capable of discussing other things, it's impossible to have a conversation with him without it moving back periodically toward something having to do with food.<br /> <br /> He's only slightly crazy, though, and realizes that as much as he would like to be able to use his special spoon, he now only saves it for very special dishes. He sees his occult knowledge in mercenary terms, as he came by it mostly by accident, and is willing to trade it to nearly anyone in return for favors, protection, or rare ingredients.<br /> <br /> In order to protect himself from enemies in the Pine Barrens, over the years Montserrat has ensorcelled numerous mortals to use as screens for his activity. Right now, his patsy is Herbert Lowenstein, a down-on-his-luck restaurateur who is now overjoyed to have an actually successful business (thanks to Montserrat's cooking). Herbert has a modicum of business acumen, but is otherwise nothing special.<br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet</b>: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231WWtBb1QxRVdaOWc/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> <b>Notes:</b> Most importantly, the magic spoon!<br /> <br /> <i>The Golden Spoon of Arcadian Delight </i>(4 dot token)<br /> <br /> <b>Mask</b>: In its mask, the golden spoon appears as nothing more than a well-used wooden kitchen spoon.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien: </b>The golden spoon is literally made out of gold (that somehow never imparts a metallic taste to the food it stirs). The very center of the ladle has a tiny garnet set into it.<br /> <br /> The Golden Spoon gives its wielder a +3 equipment bonus on any roll to make food. This ability can be used by anyone without activating its catch.<br /> <br /> Its real ability, however, is that once a year, it can make a dish whose preparation it is involved in have the same effect as consuming a Hera Pear- the eater of the food can choose any one disease he has and completely cure it.<br /> <br /> <b>Drawback: </b>Anyone who eats food prepared with the spoon has his location revealed to one of the True Fae for a year and a day. It does not just reveal the location where the food was eaten, the diner's location is constantly updated to his potential Keeper as it changes. Eating food prepared with the Golden Spoon again resets the year and a day timer.<br /> <br /> <b>Catch: </b>Non-fae can use the Hera Pear duplication effect by mixing a pint of their mother's blood in with any dish they are preparing. The Spoon can still only produce this effect once a year.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Next up will be some potential antagonists &amp; plot hooks. Got a little distracted on Wednesday since I was at my game store trawling for players- I think I've gotten a few bites.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":".  Cleaned up some typos 01-17-2015, 02:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-16T05:50:46-08:00","id":372224,"title":"","content":"Well, this is cool! But also bad. But cool! Yet also bad..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-17T11:09:39-08:00","id":373625,"title":"","content":"It feels like Montserrat could be a potential antagonist himself, albeit without him intending to be.<br /> <br /> I'm looking forward to your antagonists. I always love creating NPCs that are potential allies but I struggle with creating challenging adversaries. So I'll enjoy seeing your ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4722-birnam","name":"Birnam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4722_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T20:40:16-08:00","id":375831,"title":"","content":"Ok, I'm going to begin my &quot;potential antagonists&quot; section. I plan on making one antagonist for each Court (Sun &amp; Moon) and one unaffiliated with either. Obviously, this is a World of Darkness game, so it's entirely possible my players will not take the bait, but I do want to have some enemies handy just in case.<br /> <i>---<br /> <br /> The Dame of Wilson Street</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Ashley Dowd<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Dana Glimpse, usually just known as Glimpse<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Glimpse or Dame Glimpse, a few know her as Dana Glimpse from her day job.<br /> <br /> Titles: Dame of Wilson Avenue<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Hall &amp; Oates - Private Eyes <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCiPkazVzFA\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cCiPkazVzFA</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> Glimpse is a whipcord thin black woman in her late 30's. She's tall for a woman (about 5'7&quot;) and likes to wear stiletto heels that emphasize this. Glimpse has high cheekbones and sharp features, but her extreme (almost skeletal) thinness keep her from being considered particularly attractive in a conventional sense. She tends to wear comfortable, nondescript business-wear if she's not expecting a fight, but she always has at least one pistol secreted away on her person. Glimpse usually keeps her hair up and away from her face.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> Deep lines like circuitry, glowing with white-blue power, criss-cross Glimpse's whole body to those that can see her mien. Her eyes glow the same color. Occasionally sparks of electricity can be seen surging through her hair or across any clothing she wears.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> Spending one's teenage years in Newark in the 1990s would not have, objectively speaking, been a barrel of laughs for anyone, but Ashley Dowd managed to pull through all right. Her father was separated from her mother, but stayed in her and her two older brothers' lives. He was a handyman-for-hire and an amateur electrician for those who couldn't afford the real thing (and the union prices they charged). Her mother was a secretary and sometimes lover for a private eye who worked mostly taking pictures of cheating spouses.<br /> <br /> Ashley was relatively bright and made the best she could of Newark's abysmal government-run school system, as did her oldest brother. The middle brother, Anton, fell in with a bad crowd (as the church ladies like to say) and became a petty courier and sometimes drug peddler for the local Bloods set. A good portion of Ashely's life would likely have been spent dealing with the fallout from Anton's criminality.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, fate had a much worse future in store for Ashley. In July of 1995, she was abducted by one of the True Fae. There wasn't much to the kidnapping, and to this day she's pretty sure that she was just nabbed because she was the closest mortal at hand. She was at her father's apartment and a strange man knocked on the door, claiming to want to hire her father for some electrical work. It was late, but her father needed the money and let the stranger it. The electricity in the apartment immediately shorted out, and in the darkness the stranger -one of the True Fae or its servant- took her without much of a fight.<br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> Handed a machete and told to chop away at the Hedge, that's what Ashley did. Her Keeper was some kind of great prince (or princess, when she caught sight of it its sex sometimes changed) of storm, all great billowing clouds and booming pronouncements. It never talked to her directly and she and some of its lower-ranking servants came to call it the Thunderhead Royal. The Thunderhead Royal was a vain creature, even more vain than most True Fae, and worked its magic so that all the Changelings in its realm came to be a reflection of itself, even if their roles in its realm would have produced a different Kith or Seeming.<br /> <br /> In any event, the Thunderhead Royal had a rival that dwelt at least part of the time in the Hedge. The Thunderhead Royal's palace atop a great mountain was completely surrounded at its base by its rival's thorny domain, and he thus employed a team of Changelings to constantly hack away at the encroaching thorns. Despite being constantly at the edge of the Hedge, few of the Thunderhead Royal's &quot;pruning&quot; servants tried to escape- that area of the Hedge was under the control of the Thunderhead Royal's rival, and those few who tried to head back through to the mortal world were brutally attacked by both hobgoblins in service to the rival, and the Hedge itself. After the first two or three fellow-abductees had their heads hung on the edge of the Hedge as warnings for escapees, Ashley got the message and aborted any plans she had to escape that way.<br /> <br /> Instead, she put to use something she had learned from her mother's private-eye boss. A stakeout. It took years- to hear Ashely tell it, when she finally escaped, she was a woman in her early 70's. Every day, after her 12-hour shift of clearing away the Hedge's vines as they tried to creep up her Keeper's mountain, Ashley would explore the Fae domain, looking for good places to spy on her keeper. She surmised, correctly, that it had an alternate way of reaching the real world, since its rival kept it from making good use of the Hedge.<br /> <br /> After decades of waiting and watching, she finally found her out. Finding a hidden crevice across the ravine across from her Keeper's chambers, she spied it through a great window when it walked straight through large mirror. Wasting no time, Ashley entered the palace, almost unopposed thanks to her lowly station, and made her way as quickly as possible to the Thunderhead Royal's chambers. The guards there were more interested in keeping people out, but they made the mistake of underestimating a 70 year old woman who had spent her whole life chopping things (including the occasional hob) with a machete. Their foolishness cost them their lives, and she stepped through into the Thunderhead Royal's private rooms, and quickly found the mirror. Its glass was a swirling portal into the Hedge- a part of the Hedge far distant from the realm controlled by the rival. Without hesitation, Ashley stepped through. As she made her way back through, the Hedge stripped her of much of her time in Arcadia- including her years.<br /> <br /> When she emerged from the Hedge on the outskirts of Harrison, she was once again a young woman. The year was 2000.<br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> Despite only 5 years having passed in the real world, Ashley had lived a lifetime of hard labor in Arcadia. She looked very different from her old self, all skin and bones, even if her advanced years had been left behind in the Hedge. Assisted by some Sun Courtiers close to Chris Grand in getting back on her feet, Ashley adopted the identity of Dana Glimpse. As Glimpse, Dana approached her mother's old Private Eye employer with an offer to buy out his business. The aging detective agreed, and Glimpse purchased the service from him with a loan secured from her Modernist Sun courtier contacts.<br /> <br /> The newly re-branded Glimpse Detective Agency was born, with the nominal goal of continuing the standard cheating-spouse private eye business. In the Freehold, Glimpse offered her services as an investigator, and eventual killer, of fetches. One of the first to go was her own, though it was something of a soft target. The person that had abducted her had killed her father in the process, and left the sobbing fetch behind. With nothing else to go on for a possible suspect, the fetch was convicted of murder and sent away to prison for a long time. It wasn't that hard to bribe some of the prisoners to both &quot;shank&quot; the fetch and some of the guards to look the other way.<br /> <br /> Over the past 15 years, Glimpse and her detective agency have become the premier fetch-killers in the Freehold, and in 2012 Chris Grand granted her the title of Dame of Wilson Street (where the agency is located in Newark) in recognition for her services.<br /> <br /> All of this is only the surface, however. The Glimpse Detective Agency (which now includes Glimpse herself and two other Changelings in a motley) is more than just a fetch-killing service. It is one of the most successful Militias in the Freehold. Glimpse and her motley-mates hate almost everything having to do with the Faerie. They have, using their excellent detective skills, over the years framed many of their fellow Changelings for loyalism if they felt they were too close to their fey natures. Of course they were lauded for protecting the Freehold in this way, even though the Changelings in question were not loyalists at all, and simply &quot;too fae&quot; for Glimpse and her friends.<br /> <br /> In addition to this, Glimpse and her fellows engage in more typical Militia activities, such as directing patsies to &quot;flush out&quot; True Fae sympathizers, sending people too inexperienced to survive into the Hedge to fight hobgoblins or servants of the True Fae, and sometimes even provoking attacks or plots by the True Fae in order to then expose or stop them themselves. Of course such plots usually have a few Changeling or human casualties before the Glimpse Detective Agency can arrive to put a stop to the nefarious dealings.<br /> <br /> Glimpse is not without enemies, however. Chernobogist Moon Courtiers, who often dwell or spend a large amount of time in the Hedge, loathe her as they are frequently targets of her set-ups. Orthodox Sun courtiers, who normally would be simply cool to her because she has personal and not ideological connections to the Modernist Sun, are also suspicious of her. More than a few of their number with a mystical bent have been the subject of her crooked &quot;investigations.&quot;<br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> In person, Glimpse is a fast-talking, hard nosed gumshoe. She comes off as brusque, but is rarely rude and unlike many in her profession, does not curse like a sailor. Changelings who grew up in the 1990's liken her demeanor to Lynne Thigpen's character &quot;The Chief&quot; on the old TV show <i>Where in the World is Carmen Sandiego. </i>All in all, she's quite likable in her own way.<br /> <br /> Glimpse nurses a dark bitterness in her heart, bitterness for the True Fae for taking her away from her family and subjecting her to, in her experience, over 40 years of hard labor. Unlike most Changelings, Glimpse did not deal with this bitterness in a healthy way. There's very little that she will not do if she thinks it will be a blow against the True Fae or their influence in the world, and in her own way she's also become addicted to the adulation that her &quot;successes&quot; in protecting the community brings her.<br /> <br /> <b>\u200bCharacter Sheet</b>: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231WDVWT3Nycm9DRjQ/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a><br /> <br /> <b>Notes:</b> I think everything is pretty self-explanatory.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T20:56:32-08:00","id":375859,"title":"","content":"She's one of my favourite characters, so far. She does sound very charismatic, as you pointed out. The Motley could very easily end up working for her, I think, unaware of her more extreme methods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4722-birnam","name":"Birnam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4722_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"42"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-22T21:16:14-08:00","id":378709,"title":"","content":"Hey everyone. I have some preliminary work on the next antagonist, but I'm going away to MAGfest over the weekend so I won't be able to work on him. Putting up what I have so far and will fill it in next week. You might be able to guess where I am going <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <i>The Viscount Tumbledown</i><br /> <br /> Real Name: Peter McCloskey<br /> <br /> Name used amongst humans: Edward &quot;Ed&quot; Tumbledown, Dr. Tumbledown<br /> <br /> Name used amongst Changelings: Viscount Tumbledown, sometimes Dr. Tumbledown<br /> <br /> Titles: Viscount Tumbledown<br /> <br /> Theme Song: Nox Arcana - No Rest for the Wicked <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN4X4m5oOJA\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vN4X4m5oOJA</a><br /> <br /> <b>Mask:</b> A middle-aged man with a high forehead and deep wrinkle lines above his mouth, Peter McCloskey is mostly unremarkable. It's when he's dressed as his signature character, Dr. Tumbledown, that his becomes immediately known. In character he wears a crazy wig that makes his hair look like it was just blown out by an explosion, and a his signature neon yellow lab coat.<br /> <br /> <b>Mien:</b> In his real form, the crazy hair normally found only on his wig remains. The hair takes a riot of ever changing neon colors, as do his eyes. Tumbledown's canines grow slightly larger and point out of his mouth, while his ears grow pointed like those of an elf. Similar to a werewolf in <i>Dalu </i>form, Tumbledown is slightly more hirsute than should be normal for human, and like the hair on his head, the body hair come in a variety of bright colors.<br /> <br /> <b>Early Life:</b> <br /> <br /> <b>Durance:</b> <br /> <br /> <b>Changeling Life:</b> <br /> .<br /> <b>Personality &amp; Motivations:</b> <br /> <br /> <b>Character Sheet: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4sXzOSHZ231aWt3bXRZZ3FOeE0/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4s...ew?usp=sharing</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>Notes: </b>I will do a whole post on house rules late, but one rule I've decided on is that, since Sun and Moon don't have their own contracts, I will make Seasonal contracts available to them on a limited basis.<br /> <br /> Sun Courtiers may choose one type of contract from either Summer or Winter courts, while Moon Courtiers may choose one type of contract from either Spring or Autumn courts. That contract is an affinity contract for them and does not require any mantle or goodwill to use.<br /> <br /> This is why the Viscount has Eternal Autumn, which is his selected contract.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://paizo.com/campaigns/RageAcrossIreland/gameplay\" target=\"_blank\">My W20 Play-by-Post Game</a>","edited":{"userid":2760,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","user":"Formosus","date":". 02-03-2015, 10:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2760-formosus","name":"Formosus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2760_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"59"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}